export const AI_CONTEXT = "You are an expert on Salesforce Salesforce Nonprofit Cloud (NONPROFITCLOUD) Spring '26.\nAnswer questions based ONLY on the following product knowledge. If the question is not about NONPROFITCLOUD or you are unsure, say so clearly. Keep answers concise (2-4 sentences).\nWhen mentioning domains or components, include markdown links using the URLs from the Links section below.\n\n## Domains (20)\n- **Constituents** (constituents): The Constituents domain provides the shared foundation for people and organizations that interact with Nonprofit Cloud, including donors, volunteers, board members, program participants, case subjects, grant applicants, and partner organizations.\n- **Households & Groups** (households): Households & Groups models households and other affiliations through Party Relationship Groups and membership relationships in Nonprofit Cloud.\n- **Relationships** (relationships): Relationships in Nonprofit Cloud track connections among constituents, including individual-to-individual, individual-to-organization, and organization-to-organization ties.\n- **Contact Information** (contact_points): Contact Information in Nonprofit Cloud manages address, email, and phone records associated with constituent accounts.\n- **Fundraising Pipeline** (fundraising): The Fundraising Pipeline domain manages cultivation and solicitation for major gifts, inbound grants, and other significant donor asks in Nonprofit Cloud before they are formalized as gifts.\n- **Gift Commitments** (gift_commitments): Gift Commitments in Nonprofit Cloud manage donor promises for pledges, multipayment gifts, recurring donations, and in-kind contributions.\n- **Gift Transactions** (gift_transactions): Gift Transactions in Nonprofit Cloud record received gifts and payments as individual transactions, including one-time donations, installments that fulfill gift commitments, and matching gifts linked to their initial donations.\n- **Gift Entry & Processing** (gift_entry): Gift Entry & Processing provides Nonprofit Cloud interfaces and batch workflows for recording, validating, and processing donations.\n- **Gift Designations** (designations): Gift Designations control how donation amounts are allocated to specific funds and purposes in Nonprofit Cloud.\n- **Soft Credits** (soft_credits): Soft Credits in Nonprofit Cloud provide a consistent way to recognize constituents who influenced a gift without treating them as the primary donor.\n- **Campaigns & Outreach** (campaigns): Campaigns & Outreach manages multichannel fundraising efforts in Nonprofit Cloud by connecting campaigns and Outreach Source Codes with donor engagement and Fundraising records.\n- **Program Management** (programs): Program Management in Nonprofit Cloud tracks mission-driven programs, their benefits, constituent participants, enrollments, cohorts, schedules, sessions, goals, and benefit delivery.\n- **Case Management** (case_management): Case Management in Nonprofit Cloud equips case managers, social workers, and program staff with tools for referral intake, assessment, case follow-up, and personalized care planning.\n- **Outcome Management** (outcomes): Outcome Management in Nonprofit Cloud defines an impact strategy or theory of change, collects measurement data, tracks progress, and evaluates program effectiveness.\n- **Volunteer Management** (volunteers): Volunteer Management in Nonprofit Cloud streamlines the volunteer lifecycle from opportunity planning and recruitment through scheduling, hour tracking, and reporting.\n- **Grantmaking** (grantmaking): Grantmaking is an optional Nonprofit Cloud capability for planning, managing, delivering, and measuring outbound grants of currency or goods.\n- **Agentforce & Einstein AI** (agentforce): Agentforce & Einstein AI brings generative and conversational AI capabilities to Nonprofit Cloud across fundraising, program and case management, grantmaking, and volunteer management.\n- **Action Plans** (action_plans): Action Plans in Nonprofit Cloud define and execute the tasks required for repeatable business processes.\n- **Security & Access** (security): Security & Access manages licenses, permission assignments, and record access for Nonprofit Cloud.\n- **Setup & Configuration** (setup): Setup & Configuration is the central administration area for enabling and configuring Nonprofit Cloud capabilities, including Fundraising, Program Management, Volunteer Management, Case Management, Outcome Management, and optional Grantmaking.\n\n## Key Components (78)\n- **Person Accounts** [Constituents]: Person Accounts represent individual constituents in Nonprofit Cloud, including donors, program participants, volunteers, and other stakeholders. Nonprofit Cloud uses Person Accounts to represent individuals and can connect them to households, organizations, and other groups through Group Membership records. A Person Account can serve as the shared constituent record for fundraising, program delivery, case management, volunteer management, and grantmaking. Account pages can also present a chronological Timeline and permanent, searchable Interaction Summaries for stakeholder engagement.\n- **Business Accounts** [Constituents]: Business Accounts represent organizations, households, and other groups in Nonprofit Cloud. Use Business Accounts for organizational constituents and for households or other groups in the Nonprofit Cloud account model. Grantmaking organizations that make most grants to organizations can use the Contact plus Business Account model instead of enabling Person Accounts. Account information can also be used in grant applications and funding award summaries to identify an applicant or recipient.\n- **Contact Profiles** [Constituents]: Contact Profiles extend constituent information with fundraising-oriented attributes in Nonprofit Cloud. Use a Contact Profile to maintain fundraising-specific information for a constituent associated with an Account. The profile can capture donor classification and recurring donor type without replacing the constituent’s core Account information. This separation lets fundraising processes use specialized attributes while other Nonprofit Cloud features continue to reference the same constituent.\n- **Account Hierarchies** [Constituents]: Account Hierarchies organize related Account records through parent-child account relationships. Use the Account ParentId relationship to place an Account beneath another Account in an account hierarchy. This structure is appropriate when the relationship between the records is organizational or hierarchical. Business Accounts can represent households and other groups within the configured Nonprofit Cloud account model.\n- **Party Relationship Groups** [Households & Groups]: Party Relationship Groups provide a flexible way to represent households and other affiliations in Nonprofit Cloud. Nonprofit Cloud uses Person Accounts to represent individuals and Business Accounts to represent households and other groups. A Party Relationship Group identifies a named group, associates it with an account, and records its type. Start and end dates support groups whose relevance or composition changes over time.\n- **Household Membership** [Households & Groups]: Household Membership connects an individual to a household represented by a Party Relationship Group. Create household membership by connecting an individual to the account associated with the household group. The Account Contact Relationship identifies the account and contact and records whether the membership is active. Start and end dates preserve the period during which the membership applies.\n- **Household Addressing** [Households & Groups]: Household Addressing supports the use of household and constituent address information for outreach, including campaign mailings. Address management keeps constituent contact information accurate and organized. For constituents, use contact fields such as Mailing Address; for business contacts, use account fields such as Billing Address. Household records can provide shared contact information while individual records retain constituent-specific details.\n- **Contact Contact Relationships** [Relationships]: Contact Contact Relationships record connections between individual constituents, including spouses, parents and children, and colleagues. Use Contact Contact Relationships to model personal connections between constituents. A relationship links the contact in ContactId to the contact in RelatedContactId. RoleId identifies the configured role that describes the connection, such as a family or professional relationship.\n- **Account Contact Relationships** [Relationships]: Account Contact Relationships represent connections between individuals and organizations. Use Account Contact Relationships to capture how an individual is connected to an organization. In the Nonprofit Cloud data model, Person Accounts can represent individuals, while Business Accounts can represent households, groups, and organizations. Salesforce relationship and group-membership capabilities connect individuals and organizations to one another.\n- **Account Account Relationships** [Relationships]: Account Account Relationships model connections between organizations, households, and other account-based groups. Use Account Account Relationships to record a connection between the account in AccountId and the account in RelatedAccountId. RoleId identifies the configured role that characterizes the connection. IsActive indicates whether the organizational relationship is current.\n- **Party Role Relationships** [Relationships]: Party Role Relationships define the relationship types that can be applied to connections between parties in Nonprofit Cloud. Use Party Role Relationships to establish the available relationship classifications for constituent and organization connections. Name identifies the role, while ReciprocalLabel provides the corresponding label from the other party’s perspective. RelationshipType categorizes the definition for relationship usage.\n- **Contact Point Addresses** [Contact Information]: Contact Point Addresses store mailing and physical address information associated with constituent accounts in Nonprofit Cloud. Users add an address from the Contact Point Addresses related list on an account and provide a record name, address details, usage type, and primary designation. Supported usage examples include Home, Work, Temporary, and Inactive. Seasonal start and end information can identify when an address should be used, such as for a winter home or college dormitory.\n- **Contact Point Emails** [Contact Information]: Contact Point Emails store constituent email addresses associated with accounts in Nonprofit Cloud. Users can update constituent contact information while handling requests from existing or prospective donors. ContactPointEmail records associate an email address with an account through ParentId, classify it with EmailType, and identify a preferred record with IsPrimary. This information can be reviewed alongside other donor details when matching and supporting a constituent.\n- **Contact Point Phones** [Contact Information]: Contact Point Phones store constituent telephone numbers associated with accounts in Nonprofit Cloud. Users can review and update constituent contact information while responding to existing or prospective donors. ContactPointPhone records associate a telephone number with an account through ParentId, classify it with PhoneType, and identify a preferred record with IsPrimary. The records provide structured phone information when users search for and match a donor record during an interaction.\n- **Opportunities** [Fundraising Pipeline]: Opportunities represent active fundraising asks for individual giving, major gifts, corporate sponsorships, and grants in Nonprofit Cloud. Use opportunities when the organization needs to track the solicitation that precedes a gift. Nonprofit Cloud supports tracking gifts for particular opportunities and campaigns, and a gift commitment can be connected to both records. Opportunities are especially relevant for major gifts, patterned multipayment solicitations, inbound grants, and solicited in-kind or donor-advised fund gifts.\n- **Opportunity Contact Roles** [Fundraising Pipeline]: Opportunity Contact Roles associate multiple constituent contacts with a fundraising opportunity and describe how each person participates in the solicitation. Use Opportunity Contact Roles to connect constituent contacts to an opportunity rather than representing every participant as the legal donor. Each record references one opportunity and one contact, records the contact's role, and can designate that contact as primary. This structure supports fundraising opportunities involving several people with different responsibilities or types of influence.\n- **Sales Processes** [Fundraising Pipeline]: Sales Processes define the stage values used to track opportunities through a fundraising pipeline in Nonprofit Cloud. Configure opportunity stages to represent the steps that fundraising staff use to develop and close an ask. Stage, close date, probability, and amount provide the core information needed to evaluate an opportunity's position and expected value. Fundraising analytics can then group opportunity value and donor counts by stage.\n- **Fundraising Analytics** [Fundraising Pipeline]: Fundraising Analytics provides dashboards and metrics for evaluating donor opportunities, gift activity, and campaign performance in Nonprofit Cloud. Use the Major Donor Insights dashboard in the Philanthropy and Partnerships app to analyze donor opportunities over a selected time period. The dashboard presents key fundraising metrics, opportunity value by stage, and the number of donors associated with each stage. Donor Opportunity Details help users prioritize outreach based on opportunity value, current stage, and the number of days since the last interaction.\n- **Commitment Records** [Gift Commitments]: Commitment records represent donor promises for one-time pledges, multipayment gifts, open-ended recurring gifts, and in-kind contributions in Nonprofit Cloud. Create a gift commitment to record a one-time pledge, multipayment gift, or open-ended recurring gift, including the donor’s amount, schedule, and selected gift designations. A recurring commitment uses a recurring schedule when the donor pays a set amount at defined intervals, while a nonrecurring commitment paid in one or more installments uses a custom schedule. Gift Entry and the Gift Entry Grid can also create recurring gift commitments.\n- **Commitment Schedules** [Gift Commitments]: Commitment schedules define when and how payments associated with a gift commitment are expected. For a recurring commitment, users provide a payment start date, an optional end date, a transaction period, an interval, and a recurring transaction amount. Monthly schedules can specify the day of the month on which the schedule runs. Users can also select a payment method, provide payment instrument details, and associate the schedule with a campaign.\n- **Commitment Lifecycle** [Gift Commitments]: The commitment lifecycle covers changes to gift amounts, schedules, payment methods, active periods, and status. Users can upgrade or downgrade a recurring commitment by selecting a schedule and changing its amount or other schedule information. Effective start and end dates determine the portion of the schedule affected by the change. Existing unpaid transactions with due dates in that effective period are removed when the commitment is processed so that associated records can be regenerated from the updated schedule.\n- **Change Attribution Log** [Gift Commitments]: The Change Attribution Log associates a recorded commitment change with the affected gift commitment. A GiftCommitmentChangeAttributionLog record links a change to its GiftCommitment and classifies it with a change type and change date. The record can store the per-day amount change associated with the modification. Campaign and outreach source code references provide fundraising attribution for the recorded change.\n- **Transaction Records** [Gift Transactions]: Transaction Records Gift transactions track donations received as one-time gifts or installments that fulfill gift commitments. A transaction can be attributed to a donor, gift commitment, campaign, and outreach source code. Donors can provide payment through means such as cash, checks, stocks, or other assets. Matching gifts are represented by gift transactions and can be linked to the initial donations they match.\n- **Payment Instruments** [Gift Transactions]: Payment Instruments Payment instruments store payment details used with gift commitment schedules and their associated transactions. When users select ACH or credit card as the payment method and provide at least the last four digits, Nonprofit Cloud adds a payment instrument lookup to the schedule. If a matching payment instrument does not already exist, Nonprofit Cloud creates one.\n- **Refund Processing** [Gift Transactions]: Refund Processing Refund processing returns all or part of a paid gift amount to the donor. A refund can correct a gift entry mistake or honor a donor's request to reverse a gift. Nonprofit Cloud creates the refund in association with the original gift transaction so that the complete transaction history is retained.\n- **Acknowledgments** [Gift Transactions]: Acknowledgments Nonprofit Cloud uses customizable templates and Salesforce DocGen to create personalized gift acknowledgments and tax receipts. Organizations can generate a receipt for a single donor or produce acknowledgments in batches. Generation can also update the acknowledgment status on the included gift transaction records.\n- **Gift Entry Window** [Gift Entry & Processing]: The Gift Entry Window is the standard Nonprofit Cloud form for recording a standalone gift or adding an entry to an unprocessed batch. Users open the Gift Entry Window from Gift Entries, an Account record, or an unprocessed Gift Batch. They select an individual or organizational or household gift type, then select an existing donor or add one by name or donor ID. The form also captures the gift received date, amount, and payment method, and the received date can reflect the date on a check or the date the gift was received. A saved standalone entry is processed immediately.\n- **Gift Entry Grid** [Gift Entry & Processing]: The Gift Entry Grid is a configurable tabular interface for rapidly recording a single gift or multiple gifts in a batch. Users can open the Gift Entry Grid from a gift batch, the Gift Entries list, or an Account page. A single gift starts with one row; a batch displays its expected number of rows or ten rows by default when no expected count is provided. Users can select, create, or edit a donor and then complete the remaining gift information across the row. Rows can be added or removed, although rows backed by already-created gift entry records can't be removed.\n- **Gift Batches** [Gift Entry & Processing]: Gift Batches group multiple gift entries so that Nonprofit Cloud can validate and process them together. Users create a Gift Batch and select the default or an available custom screen template. The template provides a common set and order of fields for entries in that batch. Users can also provide expected gift count and estimated value information for validation during processing. After saving the batch, they add gift entries and process the collection together.\n- **CSV Data Import** [Gift Entry & Processing]: CSV Data Import loads large volumes of donation data into Nonprofit Cloud through CSV Data Management and a Gift Batch. Users first create a Gift Batch and copy its Batch ID. After preparing the CSV file, they add a Batch ID column and place that value in every gift-entry row intended for the batch. They then import the file through CSV Data Management. This approach reduces manual entry and the associated risk of data-entry errors.\n- **Gift Designations** [Gift Designations]: Gift Designations in Nonprofit Cloud represent funds established for specific donor-directed purposes, such as relief or community programs. Create a gift designation from the Gift Designations area and provide a name that identifies the fund or purpose. Mark the designation active so that it can be allocated when gift entries are processed; new designations are saved as active even when Active isn't selected during creation. Select Default when the designation is intended to receive unrestricted gift amounts. Only one gift designation can be the organization-wide default.\n- **Transaction Designations** [Gift Designations]: Transaction Designations allocate a gift transaction across one or more donor-selected funds in Nonprofit Cloud. Users manage allocations from a gift transaction by opening Manage Gift Transaction Designations and adding or modifying rows. The combined percentages can be equal to or less than 100%, and each transaction can have up to 50 designations. If the allocations total less than 100%, Nonprofit Cloud allocates the remaining gift amount to the organization-wide default designation. To preserve accurate record rollups, users shouldn't change a gift transaction's currency after associating it with a transaction designation.\n- **Default Designations** [Gift Designations]: Default Designations define how incoming gifts inherit allocations from fundraising records in Nonprofit Cloud. From a gift commitment, users can open Manage Gift Default Designations to review defaults inherited from the associated opportunity or campaign. If neither provides a default, the organization-wide default appears with a 100% allocation. Users can add rows or change allocation ratios, provided the combined percentage is no more than 100%. Administrators can expose the Gift Default Designations related list on Campaign and Opportunity records so their allocations can be managed there.\n- **Designation Reporting** [Gift Designations]: Designation Reporting surfaces giving metrics for designated funds in the Philanthropy and Partnerships app in Nonprofit Cloud. The Philanthropy and Partnerships app provides a Gift Designation Summary on each Gift Designation page. If the summary isn't visible, the required rollup setup may not have been completed. The summary presents gift-designation metrics at a glance alongside the app's broader donor and giving metrics. Designation data can also support connected impact reporting that relates funding allocations to outcomes.\n- **Gift Soft Credits** [Soft Credits]: Gift Soft Credits recognize people, businesses, or household members who influenced a gift without identifying them as the primary donor. Users with the FundraisingAccess permission set can add a default soft credit from the Gift Default Soft Credits related list on a Gift Commitment. They select the recipient, enter either an amount or percentage, and choose the recipient’s role. A default soft credit applies to all gifts that are part of that commitment, and its percentage can be greater than 100 percent.\n- **Matching Gift Tracking** [Soft Credits]: Matching Gift Tracking uses Gift Soft Credits to recognize a constituent’s matching-related influence on a donation. To recognize a matched donor, select the soft-credit recipient and choose Matched Donor as the recipient role. Enter an amount or percentage to define the portion being credited. When added directly to a Gift Transaction, the soft credit can cover up to 100 percent of that transaction.\n- **Tribute & Memorial Gifts** [Soft Credits]: Tribute and memorial gift context is represented conservatively in the documented soft-credit scope. Organizations can create an Experience Cloud donor portal that displays information related to tributes and soft credits with other fundraising records. Experience Cloud must be licensed, enabled, and configured before community users can access the site. Administrators should review permission sets to determine which Nonprofit Cloud objects are available to portal users.\n- **Campaign Management** [Campaigns & Outreach]: Campaign Management organizes mass-market and multichannel fundraising efforts in Nonprofit Cloud. The Donor Engagement app provides access to campaigns, outreach source codes, gift commitments, reports, and other engagement data used for mass-market fundraising. Campaigns identify the fundraising efforts that generate commitments and transactions, while campaign dates, status, expected revenue, and cost information support planning and review. Fundraisers can also use interest tags on campaigns to associate outreach efforts with donor interests, preferences, and needs.\n- **Outreach Source Codes** [Campaigns & Outreach]: Outreach Source Codes identify the outreach methods used with campaigns in Nonprofit Cloud. An Outreach Source Code belongs to a campaign and records details about a particular outreach approach. Campaign managers use these records to distinguish activity by channel, message, or audience within a broader fundraising effort. Interest tags can also be used on Outreach Source Codes when that capability is enabled.\n- **Campaign Attribution** [Campaigns & Outreach]: Campaign Attribution connects fundraising results to the campaigns and outreach methods that influenced them. Users can connect a one-time, multipayment, recurring, or in-kind gift commitment to a campaign or opportunity. A campaign can also be selected on a commitment schedule, and processing the schedule creates or updates the associated gift transactions. Gift transactions can therefore retain attribution to both the originating commitment and the relevant campaign.\n- **Campaign Performance** [Campaigns & Outreach]: Campaign Performance uses summarized engagement, donation, and financial information to evaluate fundraising efforts in Nonprofit Cloud. The Outreach Summary is an example of the Related Records Detail Display available on an Outreach Source Code or Campaign page. Administrators configure the component by selecting the related object, grouping the desired standard or custom fields, and optionally applying component-visibility filters. A Campaign Summary can include Start Date, Gift Count, Response Rate, Actual Cost in Campaign, and Average Gift Amount.\n- **Programs** [Program Management]: Programs represent the mission-driven services and initiatives that an organization delivers through Nonprofit Cloud. Program Management provides a data model for tracking programs, benefits, and benefit disbursements in Nonprofit Cloud. A program record captures the offering that an organization operates, including its summary, status, and relevant dates. Staff can use Path on Program records to guide users through a defined business process and highlight important information at each stage.\n- **Benefits & Services** [Program Management]: Benefits & Services define the classes, workshops, coaching, goods, or other assistance delivered through programs in Nonprofit Cloud. A Benefit record represents a service or good delivered through a program. Organizations can create Benefit Types to track similar benefits across programs and use a unit of measure to express the quantity delivered. For example, a distribution benefit can be measured in kits, while coaching and hands-on training can be measured in hours.\n- **Program Enrollment** [Program Management]: Program Enrollment connects participants, who are constituents, to the programs they receive through Nonprofit Cloud. Program staff create enrollments for participants who are represented in Salesforce as accounts or contacts. An enrollment identifies the participant and program and tracks the enrollment status and relevant dates. Path can guide users through the enrollment process by highlighting key fields and providing customized guidance at each step.\n- **Benefit Disbursement** [Program Management]: Benefit Disbursement records the quantity of a benefit delivered or expected to be delivered to a participant or anonymous recipient. For ad hoc delivery, staff select the relevant benefit and record the recipients who received it. The school kit distribution example uses program enrollment recipients for enrolled children and an anonymous recipient type for visitors who aren't enrolled. Salesforce creates benefit assignments and disbursements for each benefit participant in that process.\n- **Goal Definitions** [Program Management]: Goal Definitions provide reusable goals that describe the results program participants are expected to pursue. Organizations can create a hierarchy of Goal Definition records to represent progressive participant objectives. In the job readiness example, the top goal is being hired for a job, supported by intermediate goals for communication improvement and technical proficiency. Each definition provides a named and described objective that can be organized under a parent goal.\n- **Case Referral Intake** [Case Management]: Case Referral Intake supports inbound and outbound participant referrals in Nonprofit Cloud. Case Referral Intake gives case managers and program staff a structured way to process referral requests. It supports referrals into and out of the organization and records the referral type, referring organization, reason, and status. A referral can be associated with a case so that staff can continue assessment and follow-up in a managed case process.\n- **Care Plans** [Case Management]: Care Plans in Nonprofit Cloud provide personalized plans that help program participants address challenges and pursue life goals. Care Plans provide a structured way to create, manage, and share individualized plans for program participants. Each plan can identify the participant account and related case, track status, and define start and end dates. Plans can support the transition from case work into program participation and services.\n- **Assessments & Discovery** [Case Management]: Assessments & Discovery supports the collection and evaluation of participant information during case intake and ongoing service delivery. Case Management equips staff with assessment tools as part of intake and guided care planning. A Discovery Framework identifies the assessment definition, description, active status, and scoring method. This structure supports consistent discovery of information needed to understand a participant’s circumstances.\n- **Interaction Summaries** [Case Management]: Interaction Summaries capture permanent, detailed notes from meetings and conversations with participants, caseworkers, advisors, partners, and other stakeholders. Interaction Summaries provide structured, searchable records of stakeholder interactions. Caseworkers can preserve detailed notes, record next steps, and quickly locate information from earlier conversations. Confidentiality settings help protect sensitive notes while keeping relevant information available to authorized users.\n- **Outcome Definitions** [Outcome Management]: Outcome Definitions represent the intended results in an organization's impact strategy or theory of change. Use Outcome Definitions to express the results that an organization intends to achieve through its programs and services. Together, these definitions form part of an impact strategy or theory of change. They establish the outcomes against which collected data and program effectiveness can be evaluated.\n- **Indicators** [Outcome Management]: Indicators specify how progress toward an Outcome Definition is measured. Create Indicators to define the measures used to track progress toward intended outcomes. Associate each indicator with an Outcome Definition so that collected values can be evaluated in the context of the organization's impact strategy. Indicators support tracking both whether programs are delivered as planned and whether the work achieves its intended outcomes.\n- **Indicator Results** [Outcome Management]: Indicator Results capture collected measurements for an Indicator over time. Create Indicator Results as outcome data is collected. Associate each result with its Indicator and record the ResultValue and MeasurementDate. CollectionContext can preserve relevant information about the circumstances in which the measurement was obtained.\n- **Impact Reporting** [Outcome Management]: Impact Reporting uses collected outcome data to communicate program progress and effectiveness. Use the data collected through Outcome Management to create reports on outcomes. Reports can show the programs being run to achieve intended outcomes, whether those programs are being delivered as planned, and whether the work is producing the intended results. Organizations can share these reports with the communities they serve, their boards, donors, and other relevant stakeholders.\n- **Volunteer Initiatives** [Volunteer Management]: Volunteer Initiatives organize the volunteer work that helps an organization accomplish its goals and support its programs. Create a volunteer initiative to represent a project or other organized volunteer effort. Record its applicable details, including start and end dates, the number of volunteers needed, and the total volunteer hours to be filled. An initiative can also be associated with a parent initiative when it is part of a larger effort.\n- **Positions & Job Positions** [Volunteer Management]: Positions define reusable functional roles characterized by specific duties, responsibilities, skills, and qualifications. Create Positions for generic types of volunteer work that can be reused across initiatives. A Position can describe the role and identify the skills or qualifications required to hold it. If internal review is required, the Position can participate in the approval process configured for Volunteer Management.\n- **Shifts & Assignments** [Volunteer Management]: Job Position Shifts represent specific periods of volunteer work associated with a Job Position or recurrence schedule. Create an individual shift from the Job Position Shifts page, or create multiple one-time or recurring shifts from a Job Position. Each scheduled shift includes a start time, end time, and maximum number of volunteers. Recurring schedules can generate shifts according to the configured recurrence and ending criteria.\n- **Volunteer Matching** [Volunteer Management]: Volunteer Matching searches for suitable volunteers based on qualifications, shift or date-and-time criteria, and location. Configure volunteer details before running a search by creating Person Accounts and adding their location availability and competencies. A Person Competency must have a Qualification Level to appear as a match because matching evaluates that integer rather than the Proficiency Level value. Examination results can document licenses or permits, but examinations aren't evaluated by volunteer matching.\n- **Volunteer Portal** [Volunteer Management]: The Volunteer Portal uses Experience Cloud to provide self-service access to volunteer opportunities and tasks. Enable Digital Experiences and create an Experience Cloud site to establish a volunteer portal. Publish Volunteer Initiatives that should be available to portal users. Volunteers can use the site to view and sign up for available opportunities.\n- **Funding Opportunities** [Grantmaking]: Funding Opportunities define available outbound funding and the application details prospective applicants need. Funding Opportunities help grantmakers plan and communicate available grants of currency or goods. Each opportunity can capture its name, description, available funding amount, application deadline, eligibility criteria, and status. The opportunity provides context for the applications, reviews, budgets, awards, and evaluation activities that follow.\n- **Grant Applications** [Grantmaking]: Grant Applications capture an applicant organization’s request for funding, including the requested amount and project narrative. A GrantApplication connects a funding request to a FundingOpportunity and an applicant organization account. It records the requested amount, project narrative, submission date, and application status. Grantmakers can also track the organizations and people involved throughout the application process, with organization relationships providing additional context for grant decisions.\n- **Grant Budgets & Awards** [Grantmaking]: Grant Budgets & Awards support the approval, delivery, and ongoing evaluation of outbound funding. Nonprofit Cloud supports the review and approval of budgets submitted with grant applications. Budget records can identify a category, requested amount, approved amount, and justification for the request. This structure lets grantmakers preserve both the applicant’s proposal and the amount ultimately approved.\n- **Grantee Portal** [Grantmaking]: The Grantee Portal uses an Experience Cloud site template to support online communication and collaboration between grantmakers, applicants, and grantees. Nonprofit Cloud provides a site template for Experience Cloud that helps organizations create an online grantee portal. The portal gives grant seekers a place to learn about available funding opportunities and submit applications. It also supports budget submission and the upload of documents required for the funding request.\n- **AI Gift Proposals** [Agentforce & Einstein AI]: AI Gift Proposals brings generative and conversational AI to major-gift fundraising in Nonprofit Cloud. AI Gift Proposals uses Einstein Copilot and the Copilot Actions: Fundraising Gift Proposals capability to produce draft gift proposals. Major gift officers can ask Einstein Copilot to create a proposal grounded in their organization’s Salesforce data. The generated draft is intended to help officers develop compelling, personalized solicitations.\n- **Einstein Summaries** [Agentforce & Einstein AI]: Einstein Summaries uses Salesforce data to draft program and benefit summaries, notes summaries, funding award summaries, and streamlined grant applications for board review. Einstein Program and Benefits Summary drafts updates about a program and its benefits for internal and external stakeholders. Einstein Notes Summary condenses information about constituents, participants, or grantees and can surface actions or tasks recorded in notes that remain incomplete. The grantmaking capabilities summarize funding awards to help officers assess milestones and requirements, and create distilled versions of grant applications for board review.\n- **Volunteer AI Agent** [Agentforce & Einstein AI]: Volunteer AI Agent helps Nonprofit Cloud teams manage volunteer initiatives and shifts with less administrative effort. The Volunteer Management Agent supports work involving volunteer shifts and initiatives. In Agent Builder, teams can access the Shift Capacity & Coverage subagent to identify shifts that still need coverage. The subagent then helps find the most suitable volunteers for those shifts.\n- **Fundraising AI Agent** [Agentforce & Einstein AI]: Fundraising AI Agent applies Agentforce to fundraising initiatives and donor-management workflows in Nonprofit Cloud. Philanthropic Research subagents help fundraising teams optimize donor research and engagement. Teams can use the resulting insights to tailor outreach, develop personalized engagement strategies, and build deeper donor relationships. This research support complements campaign insights used to guide fundraising activity.\n- **Action Plan Templates** [Action Plans]: Action Plan Templates capture the repeatable tasks and other items required to complete a business process in Nonprofit Cloud. Action plans define the tasks and other items needed to complete a business process. Action Plan Templates capture these repeatable requirements so that staff do not have to define the same process for each record. Nonprofit Cloud documentation identifies capital campaigns, research and development projects, other fundraising activities, and volunteer initiatives as examples of processes that can use action plans.\n- **Action Plan Execution** [Action Plans]: Action Plan Execution applies a template-defined process to a target record and creates the tasks needed to perform that process. An action plan associates a selected template with a target record and a start date. The template items provide the repeatable task structure for the resulting plan. Each generated action plan item records its assignee, due date, status, and priority.\n- **Stage Management** [Action Plans]: Stage Management tracks the progression of supported Nonprofit Cloud records through defined stages and internal processes. Stage Management can track Volunteer Initiative, Job Position Assignment, Job Position Shift, Job Position, Position, and Application Form records. For Volunteer Initiatives, stages can follow progress and internal approvals from upcoming and in progress through completion. For Job Position Shifts, stages can help staff prepare for a shift and track whether it is upcoming or completed.\n- **Permission Set Groups** [Security & Access]: Permission set groups consolidate managed Nonprofit Cloud permissions with organization-specific additions or restrictions. Nonprofit Cloud provides managed permission sets for its features, and permission set groups can package the appropriate access for users. For example, users of Fundraising receive the applicable Fundraising permission set groups. Assignments can be made to a single user or multiple users through permission set assignment management.\n- **Permission Set Licenses** [Security & Access]: Permission set licenses provide prerequisites for access to certain Salesforce capabilities used with Nonprofit Cloud. Some Nonprofit Cloud configurations depend on permission set licenses in addition to permission sets or permission set groups. Gift acknowledgment setup requires Salesforce administrators to have the DocGen Designer permission set license and the SalesforceCRM Content User license. All users who generate documents receive the DocGen User permission set.\n- **Data Security & Privacy** [Security & Access]: Data security and privacy controls determine who can access Nonprofit Cloud records and fields, including constituent and case data. For Fundraising objects, administrators can set organization-wide sharing defaults to Public Read Only or, when broader access is appropriate, Public Read/Write. API integrations that access Fundraising objects use an API Integration user assigned the Fundraising Access permission set. Experience Cloud users can also receive permission sets that provide access to selected Fundraising objects and components.\n- **Custom & Muting Permission Sets** [Security & Access]: Custom permission sets add access required by organization-specific customizations, such as added fields. When an organization customizes a Nonprofit Cloud feature, administrators create a custom permission set for the additional permissions. Added fields are one documented reason to create this type of permission set. The custom permission set is then included in a permission set group with the appropriate managed permission set.\n- **Feature Enablement** [Setup & Configuration]: Feature Enablement controls the foundational capabilities used across Nonprofit Cloud. Nonprofit Cloud commonly uses Person Accounts to represent individuals, Business Accounts to represent households and other groups, and Group Membership records to connect individuals and organizations. Person Accounts are strongly recommended for organizations using Fundraising or Program Management, but they aren’t required for Grantmaking. Grantmakers that primarily fund organizations and don’t use other Nonprofit Cloud feature areas can instead use the Contact and Business Account model. Because Person Accounts can’t be turned off after activation, administrators must evaluate the account model before enabling them.\n- **Fundraising Settings** [Setup & Configuration]: Fundraising Settings controls gift-processing behavior, donor lookup and matching, gift entry, and selected fundraising capabilities in Nonprofit Cloud. Installment Extension Day Count determines how many days before or after a due date an unpaid gift commitment transaction can match a received gift as an installment. Setting the count to zero creates an exact-date matching window and can incorrectly cause unpaid gifts to become lapsed. Lapsed Unpaid Transaction Count specifies how many sequential unpaid installments change a commitment to Lapsed, while Failing Transaction Count controls when sequential failed installments change it to Failing. A value of zero for either status threshold prevents that status update.\n- **Data Processing Engine** [Setup & Configuration]: Data Processing Engine transforms data in a Nonprofit Cloud org and writes results that create or update records. Data Processing Engine requires Data Pipelines and uses definitions to transform source data into calculated results. Nonprofit Cloud uses it to summarize information about donors, volunteers, fundraising efforts, and programs. These summaries support rollup fields and Actionable Segmentation. Users running definitions must have access to every object and field referenced by the definition.\n- **Picklists & Record Types** [Setup & Configuration]: Picklists & Record Types adapts Nonprofit Cloud records to an organization’s fundraising processes. Fundraising administrators can create Payment Method picklist values to reflect the methods accepted by the organization. For in-kind gifts, they can review and modify Gift Vehicle picklist values on the Gift Commitment object. Gift entry custom-field mapping supports single-select picklist fields when the source and destination fields have compatible data types. Each mapping must use a unique source field and destination field.\n\n## Key Objects (73)\n### Constituents\n- **Account**: Account is the central constituent record for individuals represented as Person Accounts and for organizations, households, or other groups represented as Business Accounts.\n- **Contact**: Contact stores an individual associated with an Account, including FirstName, LastName, Email, and Birthdate.\n- **ContactProfile**: ContactProfile stores fundraising-specific information related to a constituent Account.\n### Households & Groups\n- **PartyRelationshipGroup**: PartyRelationshipGroup represents a named household or other group associated with an account.\n- **AccountContactRelationship**: AccountContactRelationship connects a contact to an account for household or other group membership.\n### Relationships\n- **ContactContactRelationship**: ContactContactRelationship connects the contact in ContactId with the contact in RelatedContactId and uses RoleId to describe their relationship.\n- **AccountAccountRelationship**: AccountAccountRelationship connects the account in AccountId with the account in RelatedAccountId and uses RoleId to classify the connection.\n- **PartyRoleRelationship**: PartyRoleRelationship defines an available relationship role using Name, ReciprocalLabel, and RelationshipType.\n### Contact Information\n- **ContactPointAddress**: ContactPointAddress stores a named address associated with an account through ParentId.\n- **ContactPointEmail**: ContactPointEmail stores a named email address associated with an account through ParentId.\n- **ContactPointPhone**: ContactPointPhone stores a named telephone number associated with an account through ParentId.\n### Fundraising Pipeline\n- **Opportunity**: Opportunity is the standard Salesforce record used to track a fundraising ask through its solicitation pipeline.\n- **OpportunityContactRole**: OpportunityContactRole links a constituent contact to an Opportunity and records that contact's participation in the ask.\n### Gift Commitments\n- **GiftCommitment**: GiftCommitment represents a donor’s promised gift and records its Name, AccountId, CommitmentType, Status, TotalAmount, and StartDate.\n- **GiftCommitmentSchedule**: GiftCommitmentSchedule defines payment timing for a commitment through GiftCommitmentId, Frequency, InstallmentAmount, DayOfMonth, EffectiveStartDate, and EffectiveEndDate.\n- **GiftCommitmentChangeAttributionLog**: GiftCommitmentChangeAttributionLog records an attributed change to a commitment through GiftCommitmentId, ChangeType, ChangeDate, and PerDayAmountChange.\n### Gift Transactions\n- **GiftTransaction**: GiftTransaction records an individual donation or payment and identifies its donor account, amount, status, received date, payment method, and gift vehicle.\n- **PaymentInstrument**: PaymentInstrument stores payment information associated with an account, including the payment type, last four digits, expiration date, and active status.\n### Gift Entry & Processing\n- **GiftEntry**: GiftEntry stores donation information awaiting or undergoing processing, including Name, DonorAccountId, Amount, PaymentMethod, and optional GiftBatchId.\n- **GiftBatch**: GiftBatch groups gift entries for validation and processing under the template identified by GiftBatchTemplateId.\n- **GiftBatchTemplate**: GiftBatchTemplate defines the fields and presentation used when entering gifts for a batch.\n### Gift Designations\n- **GiftDesignation**: GiftDesignation represents a named fund or purpose to which gifts can be allocated.\n- **GiftTransactionDesignation**: GiftTransactionDesignation links a gift transaction to a gift designation and records the allocated Amount and Percent.\n- **GiftDefaultDesignation**: GiftDefaultDesignation associates a gift designation with a parent fundraising record so that its allocation can be inherited during commitment or gift processing.\n### Soft Credits\n- **GiftSoftCredit**: GiftSoftCredit records attribute recognition for a Gift Transaction to a person, business, household member, or other account that influenced the gift.\n### Campaigns & Outreach\n- **Campaign**: Campaign represents a fundraising or outreach effort in Nonprofit Cloud and connects that effort to opportunities, gift commitments, gift transactions, Outreach Source Codes, and default designations.\n- **OutreachSourceCode**: OutreachSourceCode represents a specific outreach method associated with a campaign and is used to attribute gifts received in response to a solicitation.\n### Program Management\n- **Program**: Program represents a mission-driven service or initiative delivered by the organization.\n- **Benefit**: Benefit represents a service, good, class, workshop, coaching session, or other assistance associated with a program.\n- **BenefitType**: BenefitType classifies similar benefits so they can be tracked consistently across programs.\n- **UnitOfMeasure**: UnitOfMeasure defines how the quantity of a benefit is expressed, such as kits or hours.\n- **ProgramEnrollment**: ProgramEnrollment connects a participant account to a program and tracks participation in that offering.\n- **ProgramCohort**: ProgramCohort groups program participants for organized service delivery and reporting.\n- **ProgramCohortMember**: ProgramCohortMember associates a participant account with a program cohort.\n- **BenefitDisbursement**: BenefitDisbursement records a quantity of a benefit delivered or expected to be delivered to a participant.\n- **BenefitSchedule**: BenefitSchedule defines a recurring or time-bounded plan for delivering a benefit.\n- **BenefitSession**: BenefitSession represents a scheduled occurrence created for benefit delivery and attendance tracking.\n- **BenefitAssignment**: BenefitAssignment connects a participant account to a benefit so assigned assistance can be tracked.\n- **GoalDefinition**: GoalDefinition describes a reusable participant objective and can be organized into a hierarchy.\n### Case Management\n- **Case**: Case represents a matter that requires assessment and follow-up for a constituent.\n- **CarePlan**: CarePlan represents a personalized plan associated with a participant account and, when applicable, a case and template.\n- **CarePlanTemplate**: CarePlanTemplate defines a standardized starting point for participant care plans.\n- **CarePlanTemplateBenefit**: CarePlanTemplateBenefit associates a benefit with a Care Plan Template.\n- **CarePlanTemplateGoal**: CarePlanTemplateGoal associates a goal definition with a Care Plan Template.\n- **CaseReferral**: CaseReferral records an inbound or outbound referral and can associate it with a case for follow-up.\n- **AssessmentTask**: AssessmentTask tracks participant-specific assessment work associated with an account and Discovery Framework.\n- **DiscoveryFramework**: DiscoveryFramework defines a reusable structure for collecting and evaluating participant information.\n- **InteractionSummary**: InteractionSummary stores detailed information about a stakeholder meeting or conversation.\n### Outcome Management\n- **OutcomeDefinition**: OutcomeDefinition records an intended result within an impact strategy or theory of change.\n- **Indicator**: Indicator records a measure associated with an OutcomeDefinition.\n- **IndicatorResult**: IndicatorResult records a measured value for an Indicator on a specified date.\n### Volunteer Management\n- **VolunteerInitiative**: VolunteerInitiative represents an organized volunteer project or effort, including its Name, Description, StartDate, EndDate, Status, TargetVolunteerCount, and TargetHours.\n- **Position**: Position represents a reusable volunteer role that can be related to more specific Job Positions across multiple initiatives.\n- **JobPosition**: JobPosition represents a specific volunteer role associated with a VolunteerInitiative and, when applicable, a reusable Position.\n- **JobPositionShift**: JobPositionShift represents a scheduled period of work associated with a JobPosition.\n- **JobPositionAssignment**: JobPositionAssignment connects a volunteer account to a JobPositionShift and tracks the volunteer's participation.\n- **Competency**: Competency defines a skill that can be associated with volunteers and used as a qualification for volunteer roles.\n- **PersonCompetency**: PersonCompetency associates a volunteer account with a Competency and records the person's proficiency and relevant dates.\n- **Examination**: Examination defines an assessment that can qualify volunteers for a required or preferred license or permit.\n- **PersonExamination**: PersonExamination records an examination completed by a volunteer and the resulting qualification information.\n- **PersonLocationAvailability**: PersonLocationAvailability records location information used to help match a volunteer account to Job Positions.\n### Grantmaking\n- **FundingOpportunity**: FundingOpportunity defines an available funding program and its application details.\n- **GrantApplication**: GrantApplication records an organization’s request for a specific funding opportunity.\n- **ApplicationReview**: ApplicationReview records a reviewer’s evaluation of a GrantApplication.\n- **Budget**: Budget captures a categorized funding request associated with a GrantApplication.\n- **FundingAward**: FundingAward represents an approved grant associated with a GrantApplication and recipient AccountId.\n### Action Plans\n- **ActionPlan**: ActionPlan represents an executable plan created from an Action Plan Template for a target record.\n- **ActionPlanTemplate**: ActionPlanTemplate defines a reusable set of tasks for a business process.\n- **ActionPlanTemplateItem**: ActionPlanTemplateItem defines a task within an Action Plan Template.\n- **ActionPlanItem**: ActionPlanItem represents an individual task generated for an ActionPlan.\n- **StageDefinition**: StageDefinition defines an ordered stage for records of a specified object type.\n### Security & Access\n- **PermissionSetGroup**: PermissionSetGroup represents a consolidated assignment of related permission sets.\n- **PermissionSetLicense**: PermissionSetLicense represents a license prerequisite for permissions associated with certain Salesforce capabilities.\n\n## Custom Metadata Types (4)\n### Fundraising Pipeline\n- **FundraisingSetting__mdt** (Fundraising Settings): FundraisingSetting__mdt stores Fundraising Settings used to configure fundraising behavior in Nonprofit Cloud.\n### Program Management\n- **ProgramManagementSetting__mdt** (Program Management Settings): ProgramManagementSetting__mdt stores configuration represented by Program Management Settings for Nonprofit Cloud.\n### Setup & Configuration\n- **NonprofitCloudSetting__mdt** (Nonprofit Cloud Settings): NonprofitCloudSetting__mdt represents configuration metadata for Nonprofit Cloud settings.\n- **DataProcessingEngineDefinition__mdt** (Data Processing Engine Definitions): DataProcessingEngineDefinition__mdt represents configuration metadata associated with Data Processing Engine definitions.\n\n## Object Fields\n### Account (6 fields)\n- Name (Text): Account name; auto-populated from first/last name for Person Accounts\n- RecordTypeId (Lookup): Distinguishes Person Accounts from Business Accounts\n- ParentId (Lookup): Parent account for modeling organizational hierarchies\n- Type (Picklist): Account classification such as Donor, Foundation, Partner, or Vendor\n- BillingAddress (Text): Primary billing address compound field\n- Phone (Phone): Primary phone number for the account\n### Contact (5 fields)\n- FirstName (Text): Individual's first name\n- LastName (Text): Individual's last name\n- Email (Email): Primary email address\n- Birthdate (Date): Date of birth for age-based segmentation and reporting\n- AccountId (Lookup): The Account this Contact is associated with\n### ContactProfile (4 fields)\n- Name (AutoNumber): System-generated profile identifier\n- AccountId (Lookup): The constituent account this profile extends\n- DonorClassification (Picklist): Donor segmentation category such as Major Donor, Mid-Level, or Annual Fund\n- RecurringDonorType (Picklist): Indicates whether the constituent is a sustaining, lapsed, or prospective recurring donor\n### PartyRelationshipGroup (5 fields)\n- Name (Text): Display name for the group, often the household surname\n- Type (Picklist): Group classification such as Household, Affinity Group, or Board\n- AccountId (Lookup): The Business Account that represents this group\n- StartDate (Date): Date when the group was established\n- EndDate (Date): Date when the group was dissolved, if applicable\n### AccountContactRelationship (6 fields)\n- AccountId (Lookup): The organization or group account\n- ContactId (Lookup): The individual contact being linked to the account\n- Roles (MultiPicklist): One or more roles the individual holds in the organization (Head of Household, Member, Board Member)\n- IsActive (Checkbox): Whether the relationship is currently active\n- StartDate (Date): Date when the relationship began\n- EndDate (Date): Date when the relationship ended, if applicable\n### ContactContactRelationship (6 fields)\n- Name (AutoNumber): System-generated relationship identifier\n- ContactId (Lookup): The first individual in the relationship\n- RelatedContactId (Lookup): The second individual in the relationship\n- RoleId (Lookup): The Party Role Relationship defining the type (e.g., Spouse, Parent)\n- IsActive (Checkbox): Whether the relationship is currently active\n- StartDate (Date): Date when the relationship began\n### AccountAccountRelationship (5 fields)\n- Name (AutoNumber): System-generated relationship identifier\n- AccountId (Lookup): The first organization in the relationship\n- RelatedAccountId (Lookup): The second organization in the relationship\n- RoleId (Lookup): The Party Role Relationship defining the relationship type\n- IsActive (Checkbox): Whether the organizational relationship is currently active\n### PartyRoleRelationship (4 fields)\n- Name (Text): Forward-direction label for the relationship (e.g., Parent)\n- ReciprocalLabel (Text): Reverse-direction label (e.g., Child)\n- RelationshipType (Picklist): Category of relationship (Family, Professional, Community)\n- IsActive (Checkbox): Whether this relationship type is available for selection\n### ContactPointAddress (9 fields)\n- Name (Text): Display label for this address record\n- ParentId (Lookup): The Account this address belongs to (polymorphic)\n- AddressType (Picklist): Classification such as Home, Work, Vacation, or Other\n- Street (TextArea): Street address lines\n- City (Text): City name\n- StateCode (Picklist): State or province code\n- PostalCode (Text): Postal or ZIP code\n- CountryCode (Picklist): Country code\n- IsPrimary (Checkbox): Whether this is the preferred mailing address\n### ContactPointEmail (5 fields)\n- Name (Text): Display label for this email record\n- ParentId (Lookup): The Account this email belongs to (polymorphic)\n- EmailAddress (Email): The email address\n- EmailType (Picklist): Classification such as Personal, Work, or Other\n- IsPrimary (Checkbox): Whether this is the preferred email address\n### ContactPointPhone (5 fields)\n- Name (Text): Display label for this phone record\n- ParentId (Lookup): The Account this phone number belongs to (polymorphic)\n- TelephoneNumber (Phone): The phone number\n- PhoneType (Picklist): Classification such as Home, Mobile, Work, or Fax\n- IsPrimary (Checkbox): Whether this is the preferred phone number\n### Opportunity (8 fields)\n- Name (Text): Descriptive name for the solicitation or proposal\n- Amount (Currency): Expected gift amount for the opportunity\n- StageName (Picklist): Current pipeline stage (Prospecting, Cultivation, Solicitation, Closed Won)\n- CloseDate (Date): Expected or actual close date\n- Probability (Percent): Likelihood of winning the opportunity\n- AccountId (Lookup): The donor or organization account\n- CampaignId (Lookup): The campaign that sourced this opportunity\n- RecordTypeId (Lookup): Distinguishes major gifts, grants, planned giving, and other fundraising types\n### OpportunityContactRole (4 fields)\n- OpportunityId (Lookup): The opportunity this contact role belongs to\n- ContactId (Lookup): The individual involved in the opportunity\n- Role (Picklist): The person's role (Primary Donor, Influencer, Decision Maker, Soft Credit)\n- IsPrimary (Checkbox): Whether this is the primary contact for the opportunity\n### GiftCommitment (8 fields)\n- Name (AutoNumber): System-generated commitment identifier\n- AccountId (Lookup): The donor account making the commitment\n- CommitmentType (Picklist): Type of commitment: Recurring or Custom\n- Status (Picklist): Lifecycle status: Draft, Active, Paused, Lapsed, Failing, or Closed\n- TotalAmount (Currency): Total committed amount across all installments\n- CampaignId (Lookup): The campaign that sourced this commitment\n- OpportunityId (Lookup): The originating fundraising opportunity\n- StartDate (Date): Date when the commitment begins\n### GiftCommitmentSchedule (7 fields)\n- Name (AutoNumber): System-generated schedule identifier\n- GiftCommitmentId (Lookup): The parent gift commitment\n- Frequency (Picklist): Installment frequency: Monthly, Quarterly, Annually, or Custom\n- InstallmentAmount (Currency): Amount per installment payment\n- DayOfMonth (Number): Day of the month on which installments are due\n- EffectiveStartDate (Date): Date when this schedule becomes active\n- EffectiveEndDate (Date): Date when this schedule stops generating transactions\n### GiftCommitmentChangeAttributionLog (7 fields)\n- Name (AutoNumber): System-generated log entry identifier\n- GiftCommitmentId (Lookup): The commitment that was changed\n- ChangeType (Picklist): Type of change: Upgrade, Downgrade, Pause, Resume, Payment Method Change\n- PerDayAmountChange (Currency): Daily amount impact of the change for ROI calculation\n- CampaignId (Lookup): The campaign that influenced this change\n- OutreachSourceCodeId (Lookup): The specific outreach source code that drove the change\n- ChangeDate (DateTime): Timestamp when the change was made\n### GiftTransaction (12 fields)\n- Name (AutoNumber): System-generated transaction identifier\n- AccountId (Lookup): The donor account for this transaction\n- Amount (Currency): Transaction amount received or expected\n- Status (Picklist): Transaction lifecycle: Unpaid, Pending, Paid, Failed, Canceled, Refunded, Written Off\n- GiftReceivedDate (Date): Date the gift was received\n- PaymentMethod (Picklist): How the donor paid: Credit Card, Check, ACH, Wire, Cash\n- GiftVehicle (Picklist): Gift type: Cash, Check, Credit Card, Stock, Wire Transfer, In-Kind\n- GiftCommitmentId (Lookup): The commitment this transaction fulfills, if applicable\n- CampaignId (Lookup): Campaign attribution for this transaction\n- OutreachSourceCodeId (Lookup): Granular outreach source code for attribution\n- AcknowledgmentStatus (Picklist): Whether the donor has been thanked: Not Sent, Sent, Failed\n- MatchingEmployerTransactionId (Lookup): Links to the corporate matching gift transaction\n### PaymentInstrument (6 fields)\n- Name (Text): Display name for the payment method (e.g., Visa ending 4242)\n- AccountId (Lookup): The donor account that owns this payment instrument\n- PaymentType (Picklist): Type of payment: Credit Card, ACH, Bank Transfer\n- LastFourDigits (Text): Last four digits of the card or account number\n- ExpirationDate (Date): Expiration date for credit card instruments\n- IsActive (Checkbox): Whether this payment instrument is currently active\n### GiftEntry (7 fields)\n- Name (AutoNumber): System-generated entry identifier\n- DonorAccountId (Lookup): The donor account for this gift entry\n- Amount (Currency): Gift amount to be processed\n- PaymentMethod (Picklist): How the donor is paying\n- GiftBatchId (Lookup): The batch this entry belongs to, if applicable\n- ProcessingStatus (Picklist): Entry status: Draft, Ready, Processing, Completed, Failed\n- ProcessingResult (LongTextArea): Processing outcome details including any error messages\n### GiftBatch (6 fields)\n- Name (Text): Descriptive name for the batch\n- GiftBatchTemplateId (Lookup): The template controlling which fields appear in the batch\n- ExpectedGiftCount (Number): Expected number of gifts for reconciliation\n- EstimatedValue (Currency): Expected total value for reconciliation\n- Status (Picklist): Batch status: Open, Processing, Completed, Partially Processed\n- ProcessedDate (DateTime): Timestamp when the batch was processed\n### GiftBatchTemplate (4 fields)\n- Name (Text): Template name describing its purpose\n- Description (LongTextArea): Explanation of when to use this template\n- FieldConfiguration (LongTextArea): JSON configuration of fields, order, and defaults\n- IsActive (Checkbox): Whether this template is available for use\n### GiftDesignation (6 fields)\n- Name (Text): Fund or purpose name displayed to donors and staff\n- IsActive (Checkbox): Whether this designation is available for gift allocation\n- IsDefault (Checkbox): Whether this is the org-wide default designation for unrestricted gifts\n- TotalAmount (Currency): Rollup total of all allocated gift transaction amounts\n- TransactionCount (Number): Rollup count of gift transactions allocated to this designation\n- Description (LongTextArea): Description of the fund's purpose and restrictions\n### GiftTransactionDesignation (5 fields)\n- Name (AutoNumber): System-generated allocation identifier\n- GiftTransactionId (Lookup): The gift transaction being allocated\n- GiftDesignationId (Lookup): The designation receiving the allocation\n- Amount (Currency): Dollar amount allocated to this designation\n- Percent (Percent): Percentage of the transaction allocated to this designation\n### GiftDefaultDesignation (4 fields)\n- Name (AutoNumber): System-generated default designation identifier\n- GiftDesignationId (Lookup): The designation to use as the default\n- ParentRecordId (Lookup): The campaign, commitment, or opportunity this default applies to (polymorphic)\n- Priority (Number): Priority level in the inheritance hierarchy\n### GiftSoftCredit (6 fields)\n- Name (AutoNumber): System-generated soft credit identifier\n- GiftTransactionId (Lookup): The gift transaction being credited\n- AccountId (Lookup): The constituent account receiving the soft credit\n- CreditType (Picklist): Type of credit: Influence, Tribute, Solicitation, Household Member, Matching\n- Amount (Currency): Amount of the soft credit, which may be partial\n- Description (TextArea): Notes about why this credit was given\n### Campaign (8 fields)\n- Name (Text): Campaign name\n- Type (Picklist): Campaign type: Appeal, Event, Direct Mail, Digital, Phonathon\n- Status (Picklist): Campaign lifecycle: Planned, In Progress, Completed, Aborted\n- StartDate (Date): Campaign start date\n- EndDate (Date): Campaign end date\n- BudgetedCost (Currency): Planned budget for the campaign\n- ExpectedRevenue (Currency): Expected total revenue from the campaign\n- ActualCost (Currency): Actual cost incurred\n### OutreachSourceCode (6 fields)\n- Name (Text): Source code identifier, often a formatted tracking code\n- CampaignId (Lookup): The parent campaign\n- Channel (Picklist): Communication channel: Email, Direct Mail, Social, Phone, Web\n- Message (Text): Specific message or creative identifier\n- Audience (Text): Target audience segment description\n- IsActive (Checkbox): Whether this source code is active for attribution\n### Program (5 fields)\n- Name (Text): Program name\n- Summary (LongTextArea): Detailed description of the program's purpose and activities\n- Status (Picklist): Program lifecycle: Active, Inactive, Planned, Completed\n- StartDate (Date): Date when the program begins operations\n- EndDate (Date): Date when the program concludes, if applicable\n### Benefit (5 fields)\n- Name (Text): Benefit name describing the service or good\n- ProgramId (Lookup): The program that offers this benefit\n- BenefitTypeId (Lookup): The type category: Coaching, Distribution, Training, etc.\n- UnitOfMeasureId (Lookup): The unit used to measure delivery quantity\n- IsActive (Checkbox): Whether this benefit is currently offered\n### BenefitType (3 fields)\n- Name (Text): Type name such as Coaching, Distribution, or Training\n- Description (LongTextArea): Explanation of what this benefit type covers\n- IsActive (Checkbox): Whether this type is available for classifying benefits\n### UnitOfMeasure (3 fields)\n- Name (Text): Unit name such as Hours, Sessions, Meals, or Items\n- Abbreviation (Text): Short form of the unit (e.g., hrs, qty)\n- IsActive (Checkbox): Whether this unit is available for use\n### ProgramEnrollment (6 fields)\n- Name (AutoNumber): System-generated enrollment identifier\n- AccountId (Lookup): The participant account\n- ProgramId (Lookup): The program the participant is enrolling in\n- Status (Picklist): Enrollment status: Applied, Enrolled, Active, Completed, Withdrawn\n- EnrollmentDate (Date): Date when the participant enrolled\n- CompletionDate (Date): Date when the participant completed or withdrew\n### ProgramCohort (5 fields)\n- Name (Text): Cohort name or identifier\n- ProgramId (Lookup): The program this cohort belongs to\n- StartDate (Date): Cohort start date\n- EndDate (Date): Cohort end date\n- Capacity (Number): Maximum number of participants in this cohort\n### ProgramCohortMember (4 fields)\n- Name (AutoNumber): System-generated member identifier\n- ProgramCohortId (Lookup): The cohort this member belongs to\n- AccountId (Lookup): The participant account\n- Status (Picklist): Membership status: Active, Inactive, Completed\n### BenefitDisbursement (6 fields)\n- Name (AutoNumber): System-generated disbursement identifier\n- AccountId (Lookup): The participant receiving the benefit\n- BenefitId (Lookup): The benefit being delivered\n- Quantity (Number): Amount delivered in the benefit's unit of measure\n- DisbursementDate (Date): Date of service delivery\n- Status (Picklist): Delivery status: Scheduled, Delivered, Missed, Canceled\n### BenefitSchedule (5 fields)\n- Name (Text): Schedule name describing the delivery pattern\n- BenefitId (Lookup): The benefit this schedule applies to\n- Frequency (Picklist): Delivery frequency: Daily, Weekly, Biweekly, Monthly\n- StartDate (Date): Schedule start date\n- EndDate (Date): Schedule end date\n### BenefitSession (5 fields)\n- Name (Text): Session name or date identifier\n- BenefitScheduleId (Lookup): The schedule that generated this session\n- SessionDate (DateTime): Date and time of the session\n- Location (Text): Where the session takes place\n- Status (Picklist): Session status: Scheduled, In Progress, Completed, Canceled\n### BenefitAssignment (5 fields)\n- Name (AutoNumber): System-generated assignment identifier\n- AccountId (Lookup): The participant assigned to receive this benefit\n- BenefitId (Lookup): The benefit the participant is assigned to receive\n- AssignedQuantity (Number): Total quantity the participant is expected to receive\n- Status (Picklist): Assignment status: Active, Completed, Suspended\n### GoalDefinition (5 fields)\n- Name (Text): Goal name describing the desired outcome\n- Description (LongTextArea): Detailed description of the goal and success criteria\n- ParentGoalId (Lookup): Parent goal for hierarchical goal structures\n- TargetDate (Date): Date by which the goal should be achieved\n- Status (Picklist): Goal status: Not Started, In Progress, Achieved, Not Achieved\n### Case (8 fields)\n- CaseNumber (AutoNumber): System-generated case identifier\n- Subject (Text): Brief description of the case subject\n- Status (Picklist): Case lifecycle: New, In Progress, Pending, Closed\n- Priority (Picklist): Urgency level: Low, Medium, High, Critical\n- AccountId (Lookup): The participant account\n- ContactId (Lookup): The participant contact\n- Origin (Picklist): How the case was created: Self-Referral, Partner Agency, Internal, Phone\n- Description (LongTextArea): Detailed description of the participant's needs\n### CarePlan (7 fields)\n- Name (Text): Care plan name describing its focus area\n- AccountId (Lookup): The participant account this plan serves\n- CaseId (Lookup): The case this care plan addresses\n- CarePlanTemplateId (Lookup): The template used as a starting point\n- Status (Picklist): Plan status: Draft, Active, Completed, Discontinued\n- StartDate (Date): Plan start date\n- EndDate (Date): Plan end date\n### CarePlanTemplate (3 fields)\n- Name (Text): Template name describing the scenario it addresses\n- Description (LongTextArea): Detailed description of when to use this template\n- IsActive (Checkbox): Whether this template is available for use\n### CarePlanTemplateBenefit (4 fields)\n- Name (AutoNumber): System-generated identifier\n- CarePlanTemplateId (Lookup): The parent care plan template\n- BenefitId (Lookup): The benefit to include in the plan\n- RecommendedQuantity (Number): Suggested delivery quantity for this benefit\n### CarePlanTemplateGoal (4 fields)\n- Name (AutoNumber): System-generated identifier\n- CarePlanTemplateId (Lookup): The parent care plan template\n- GoalDefinitionId (Lookup): The goal to include in the plan\n- TargetDays (Number): Recommended number of days to achieve this goal\n### CaseReferral (6 fields)\n- Name (AutoNumber): System-generated referral identifier\n- CaseId (Lookup): The case created from or associated with this referral\n- ReferralType (Picklist): Direction: Inbound or Outbound\n- ReferringOrganization (Text): The organization making the referral\n- Reason (TextArea): Why the participant is being referred\n- Status (Picklist): Referral status: Pending, Accepted, Declined, Completed\n### AssessmentTask (6 fields)\n- Name (Text): Assessment name or type\n- AccountId (Lookup): The participant being assessed\n- DiscoveryFrameworkId (Lookup): The assessment framework used\n- Status (Picklist): Assessment status: Not Started, In Progress, Completed\n- Score (Number): Calculated assessment score\n- CompletedDate (DateTime): When the assessment was completed\n### DiscoveryFramework (4 fields)\n- Name (Text): Framework name describing the assessment type\n- Description (LongTextArea): Purpose and usage instructions for this framework\n- IsActive (Checkbox): Whether this framework is available for use\n- ScoringMethod (Picklist): How scores are calculated: Sum, Average, Weighted\n### InteractionSummary (7 fields)\n- Name (Text): Interaction title or subject\n- AccountId (Lookup): The account this interaction relates to\n- InteractionType (Picklist): Type: Meeting, Phone Call, Email, Home Visit, Group Session\n- InteractionDate (DateTime): When the interaction took place\n- Notes (RichTextArea): Detailed notes from the interaction\n- NextSteps (LongTextArea): Planned follow-up actions\n- IsConfidential (Checkbox): Whether this interaction contains sensitive information requiring restricted access\n### OutcomeDefinition (5 fields)\n- Name (Text): Outcome name describing the desired impact\n- Description (LongTextArea): Detailed description of the outcome and its significance\n- TargetPopulation (Text): The group of people this outcome applies to\n- ParentOutcomeId (Lookup): Parent outcome for hierarchical theory of change\n- IsActive (Checkbox): Whether this outcome definition is currently tracked\n### Indicator (6 fields)\n- Name (Text): Indicator name describing the metric\n- OutcomeDefinitionId (Lookup): The outcome this indicator measures\n- DataType (Picklist): Data type: Number, Percent, Currency, Text\n- CollectionFrequency (Picklist): How often data is collected: Weekly, Monthly, Quarterly, Annually\n- TargetValue (Number): The target value that represents success\n- BaselineValue (Number): Starting value before program intervention\n### IndicatorResult (5 fields)\n- Name (AutoNumber): System-generated result identifier\n- IndicatorId (Lookup): The indicator this result measures\n- ResultValue (Number): The measured value\n- MeasurementDate (Date): Date when the measurement was taken\n- CollectionContext (TextArea): Notes about how and where the data was collected\n### VolunteerInitiative (9 fields)\n- Name (Text): Initiative name\n- Description (LongTextArea): Detailed description of the volunteer project\n- StartDate (Date): Initiative start date\n- EndDate (Date): Initiative end date\n- Status (Picklist): Initiative status: Planned, Active, Completed, Canceled\n- TargetVolunteerCount (Number): Target number of volunteers needed\n- TargetHours (Number): Target total volunteer hours\n- ParentInitiativeId (Lookup): Parent initiative for hierarchical structures\n- IsPublished (Checkbox): Whether this initiative is visible on the volunteer portal\n### Position (4 fields)\n- Name (Text): Position title\n- Description (LongTextArea): Role description and general responsibilities\n- RequiredQualifications (LongTextArea): Qualifications needed for this role\n- IsActive (Checkbox): Whether this position is available for use\n### JobPosition (6 fields)\n- Name (Text): Job position name specific to this initiative\n- VolunteerInitiativeId (Lookup): The initiative this job position belongs to\n- PositionId (Lookup): The generic position this job position instantiates\n- Location (Text): Where the volunteer will work\n- RequiresApproval (Checkbox): Whether signup requires manager approval\n- HeadcountTarget (Number): Number of volunteers needed for this job position\n### JobPositionShift (6 fields)\n- Name (Text): Shift name or time slot description\n- JobPositionId (Lookup): The job position this shift belongs to\n- StartDateTime (DateTime): Shift start date and time\n- EndDateTime (DateTime): Shift end date and time\n- Capacity (Number): Maximum number of volunteers for this shift\n- FilledCount (Number): Number of volunteers currently assigned\n### JobPositionAssignment (5 fields)\n- Name (AutoNumber): System-generated assignment identifier\n- JobPositionShiftId (Lookup): The shift this volunteer is assigned to\n- AccountId (Lookup): The volunteer's person account\n- Status (Picklist): Assignment status: Upcoming, In Progress, Completed, No Show, Canceled\n- ActualHours (Number): Actual hours worked by the volunteer\n### Competency (4 fields)\n- Name (Text): Competency name\n- Description (LongTextArea): What this competency represents\n- Category (Picklist): Competency category: Skill, Certification, License, Background Check\n- IsActive (Checkbox): Whether this competency is actively tracked\n### PersonCompetency (6 fields)\n- Name (AutoNumber): System-generated identifier\n- AccountId (Lookup): The volunteer who holds this competency\n- CompetencyId (Lookup): The competency being recorded\n- ProficiencyLevel (Picklist): Proficiency: Beginner, Intermediate, Advanced, Expert\n- DateAchieved (Date): When the volunteer earned this competency\n- ExpirationDate (Date): When the competency expires, if applicable\n### Examination (5 fields)\n- Name (Text): Examination name\n- Description (LongTextArea): What this examination covers and how it is administered\n- PassingScore (Number): Minimum score required to pass\n- ValidityPeriod (Number): Number of months the result remains valid\n- IsActive (Checkbox): Whether this examination is currently in use\n### PersonExamination (7 fields)\n- Name (AutoNumber): System-generated identifier\n- AccountId (Lookup): The volunteer who took the examination\n- ExaminationId (Lookup): The examination that was taken\n- Score (Number): Score achieved on the examination\n- Passed (Checkbox): Whether the volunteer passed\n- CompletedDate (Date): Date the examination was completed\n- ExpirationDate (Date): Date when this result expires\n### PersonLocationAvailability (5 fields)\n- Name (Text): Location description\n- AccountId (Lookup): The volunteer who is available at this location\n- City (Text): City where the volunteer is available\n- State (Text): State or province\n- MaxDistanceMiles (Number): Maximum distance the volunteer is willing to travel\n### FundingOpportunity (7 fields)\n- Name (Text): Funding opportunity title\n- Description (RichTextArea): Detailed description of the funding opportunity\n- FundingAmount (Currency): Total funding available for this opportunity\n- ApplicationDeadline (DateTime): Last date to submit applications\n- EligibilityCriteria (LongTextArea): Requirements applicants must meet\n- Status (Picklist): Opportunity status: Draft, Open, Closed, Awarded\n- IsPublished (Checkbox): Whether visible on the grantee portal\n### GrantApplication (7 fields)\n- Name (AutoNumber): System-generated application identifier\n- FundingOpportunityId (Lookup): The funding opportunity being applied for\n- ApplicantAccountId (Lookup): The organization submitting the application\n- RequestedAmount (Currency): Amount of funding requested\n- ProjectNarrative (RichTextArea): Description of the proposed project\n- Status (Picklist): Application status: Draft, Submitted, Under Review, Awarded, Declined\n- SubmittedDate (DateTime): When the application was submitted\n### ApplicationReview (6 fields)\n- Name (AutoNumber): System-generated review identifier\n- GrantApplicationId (Lookup): The application being reviewed\n- ReviewerId (Lookup): The user performing the review\n- Score (Number): Numeric evaluation score\n- Comments (RichTextArea): Qualitative assessment comments\n- Recommendation (Picklist): Reviewer recommendation: Approve, Decline, Revise and Resubmit\n### Budget (6 fields)\n- Name (Text): Budget name or category\n- GrantApplicationId (Lookup): The application this budget belongs to\n- Category (Picklist): Budget category: Personnel, Direct Costs, Overhead, Travel, Equipment\n- RequestedAmount (Currency): Amount requested for this budget line\n- ApprovedAmount (Currency): Amount approved for this budget line\n- Justification (LongTextArea): Narrative explaining why this cost is necessary\n### FundingAward (7 fields)\n- Name (AutoNumber): System-generated award identifier\n- GrantApplicationId (Lookup): The approved application\n- AccountId (Lookup): The grantee organization account\n- AwardAmount (Currency): Total approved funding amount\n- AwardDate (Date): Date the award was granted\n- Status (Picklist): Award status: Active, Completed, Suspended, Terminated\n- DisbursedAmount (Currency): Total amount disbursed to date\n### ActionPlan (6 fields)\n- Name (Text): Action plan name, usually derived from the template\n- ActionPlanTemplateId (Lookup): The template used to create this plan\n- TargetId (Lookup): The record this plan is applied to (polymorphic: Opportunity, Case, Initiative)\n- Status (Picklist): Plan status: Not Started, In Progress, Completed\n- StartDate (Date): Date the plan was initiated\n- CompletionPercentage (Percent): Percentage of tasks completed\n### ActionPlanTemplate (4 fields)\n- Name (Text): Template name describing the process\n- Description (LongTextArea): Detailed description of when to use this template\n- TargetObjectType (Picklist): Object type this template applies to: Opportunity, Case, Initiative\n- IsActive (Checkbox): Whether this template is available for use\n### ActionPlanTemplateItem (6 fields)\n- Name (Text): Task name\n- ActionPlanTemplateId (Lookup): The parent template\n- Description (LongTextArea): Task description and instructions\n- DaysFromStart (Number): Number of days from plan start until this task is due\n- Priority (Picklist): Task priority: Low, Normal, High\n- DependsOnItemId (Lookup): Another template item that must be completed first\n### ActionPlanItem (6 fields)\n- Name (Text): Task name from the template item\n- ActionPlanId (Lookup): The parent action plan\n- AssignedToId (Lookup): The user assigned to complete this task\n- DueDate (Date): Calculated due date based on plan start and offset\n- Status (Picklist): Task status: Not Started, In Progress, Completed\n- Priority (Picklist): Task priority: Low, Normal, High\n### StageDefinition (5 fields)\n- Name (Text): Stage name\n- ObjectType (Picklist): The object this stage applies to: Initiative, Position, Assignment\n- StageOrder (Number): Sequential order of this stage in the progression\n- Description (LongTextArea): Description of what this stage represents\n- ActionPlanTemplateId (Lookup): Template to trigger when a record enters this stage\n\n## Object Relationships\n- Account -> Contact (parent): Person Accounts have an associated Contact record that stores individual-specific fields\n- Account -> ContactProfile (parent): Accounts can have a Contact Profile with extended fundraising attributes\n- Account -> AccountContactRelationship (parent): Accounts are linked to contacts through Account Contact Relationships for household and organizational membership\n- Account -> ContactPointAddress (parent): Accounts have one or more contact point addresses\n- Contact -> Account (child): Each Contact belongs to an Account; for Person Accounts this is a one-to-one relationship\n- Contact -> ContactContactRelationship (parent): Contacts participate in interpersonal relationships\n- Contact -> OpportunityContactRole (parent): Contacts are linked to fundraising opportunities through contact roles\n- ContactProfile -> Account (child): Each Contact Profile belongs to a single constituent account\n- ContactProfile -> GiftCommitment (lookup): Contact profiles inform recurring commitment tracking and donor classification\n- PartyRelationshipGroup -> Account (child): Each group is represented by a Business Account\n- PartyRelationshipGroup -> AccountContactRelationship (parent): Group membership is tracked through Account Contact Relationship records\n- AccountContactRelationship -> Account (child): Each ACR links to the organization or group account\n- AccountContactRelationship -> Contact (child): Each ACR links to the individual contact\n- AccountContactRelationship -> PartyRelationshipGroup (lookup): ACRs connect individuals to Party Relationship Group households\n- ContactContactRelationship -> Contact (child): Links to the first individual in the relationship\n- ContactContactRelationship -> PartyRoleRelationship (child): References the party role that defines the relationship type and reciprocal labels\n- AccountAccountRelationship -> Account (child): Links to the first organization account\n- AccountAccountRelationship -> PartyRoleRelationship (child): References the party role defining the relationship type\n- PartyRoleRelationship -> ContactContactRelationship (parent): Party roles are used by Contact Contact Relationships\n- PartyRoleRelationship -> AccountAccountRelationship (parent): Party roles are used by Account Account Relationships\n- ContactPointAddress -> Account (child): Each address belongs to a constituent account\n- ContactPointAddress -> PartyRelationshipGroup (lookup): Household members may share addresses through the group\n- ContactPointEmail -> Account (child): Each email belongs to a constituent account\n- ContactPointPhone -> Account (child): Each phone number belongs to a constituent account\n- Opportunity -> Account (child): Each opportunity is associated with a donor account\n- Opportunity -> Campaign (child): Opportunities are sourced from campaigns\n- Opportunity -> OpportunityContactRole (parent): Opportunities have contact roles identifying involved individuals\n- Opportunity -> GiftCommitment (lookup): Won opportunities can become gift commitments\n- OpportunityContactRole -> Opportunity (child): Each contact role belongs to an opportunity\n- OpportunityContactRole -> Contact (child): Each contact role references an individual\n- OpportunityContactRole -> GiftSoftCredit (lookup): Contact roles can inform soft credit attribution\n- GiftCommitment -> Account (child): Each commitment belongs to a donor account\n- GiftCommitment -> GiftCommitmentSchedule (parent): Commitments have one or more schedules defining installment patterns\n- GiftCommitment -> GiftTransaction (parent): Commitments generate gift transactions for each installment\n- GiftCommitment -> Opportunity (child): Commitments can originate from fundraising opportunities\n- GiftCommitmentSchedule -> GiftCommitment (child): Each schedule belongs to a gift commitment\n- GiftCommitmentSchedule -> PaymentInstrument (lookup): Schedules reference the payment method used for installments\n- GiftCommitmentChangeAttributionLog -> GiftCommitment (child): Each log entry references the modified commitment\n- GiftCommitmentChangeAttributionLog -> Campaign (child): Changes are attributed to campaigns for ROI analysis\n- GiftTransaction -> Account (child): Each transaction belongs to a donor account\n- GiftTransaction -> GiftCommitment (child): Transactions fulfill commitment installments\n- GiftTransaction -> Campaign (child): Transactions are attributed to campaigns\n- GiftTransaction -> GiftTransactionDesignation (parent): Transactions are allocated to designations through junction records\n- GiftTransaction -> GiftSoftCredit (parent): Transactions can have soft credit recipients\n- PaymentInstrument -> Account (child): Each payment instrument belongs to a donor account\n- PaymentInstrument -> GiftCommitmentSchedule (lookup): Payment instruments are linked to commitment schedules for recurring processing\n- GiftEntry -> Account (child): Each gift entry references a donor account\n- GiftEntry -> GiftBatch (child): Entries are grouped in batches for bulk processing\n- GiftEntry -> GiftTransaction (lookup): Processing creates a gift transaction from each entry\n- GiftBatch -> GiftEntry (parent): Batches contain multiple gift entries\n- GiftBatch -> GiftBatchTemplate (child): Each batch uses a template for field configuration\n- GiftBatchTemplate -> GiftBatch (parent): Templates are applied to gift batches\n- GiftDesignation -> GiftTransactionDesignation (parent): Designations receive allocations through gift transaction designation junction records\n- GiftDesignation -> GiftDefaultDesignation (parent): Designations can be set as defaults at various levels\n- GiftTransactionDesignation -> GiftTransaction (child): Each allocation belongs to a gift transaction\n- GiftTransactionDesignation -> GiftDesignation (child): Each allocation references a gift designation fund\n- GiftDefaultDesignation -> GiftDesignation (child): Each default references a gift designation\n- GiftDefaultDesignation -> Campaign (lookup): Default designations can be set at the campaign level\n- GiftDefaultDesignation -> GiftCommitment (lookup): Default designations can be set at the commitment level\n- GiftSoftCredit -> GiftTransaction (child): Each soft credit references a gift transaction\n- GiftSoftCredit -> Account (child): Each soft credit is attributed to a constituent account\n- GiftSoftCredit -> ContactContactRelationship (lookup): Relationships between constituents inform soft credit decisions\n- Campaign -> OutreachSourceCode (parent): Campaigns contain outreach source codes for granular attribution\n- Campaign -> GiftTransaction (parent): Gift transactions are attributed to campaigns\n- Campaign -> GiftCommitment (parent): Gift commitments are linked to campaigns\n- Campaign -> Opportunity (parent): Fundraising opportunities are sourced from campaigns\n- OutreachSourceCode -> Campaign (child): Each source code belongs to a campaign\n- OutreachSourceCode -> GiftTransaction (parent): Transactions reference source codes for granular attribution\n- OutreachSourceCode -> GiftCommitmentChangeAttributionLog (parent): Commitment changes reference source codes for ROI tracking\n- Program -> Benefit (parent): Programs offer specific benefits and services to participants\n- Program -> ProgramEnrollment (parent): Participants enroll in programs\n- Program -> ProgramCohort (parent): Programs organize participants into cohorts\n- Program -> OutcomeDefinition (lookup): Programs are measured against defined outcomes\n- Benefit -> Program (child): Each benefit belongs to a program\n- Benefit -> BenefitType (child): Benefits are classified by type\n- Benefit -> BenefitDisbursement (parent): Benefits are delivered through disbursement records\n- Benefit -> BenefitSchedule (parent): Benefits have delivery schedules\n- BenefitType -> Benefit (parent): Benefit types classify one or more benefits across programs\n- UnitOfMeasure -> Benefit (parent): Units of measure are assigned to benefits for quantity tracking\n- ProgramEnrollment -> Account (child): Each enrollment references a participant account\n- ProgramEnrollment -> Program (child): Each enrollment references a program\n- ProgramEnrollment -> ProgramCohort (lookup): Enrollees can be assigned to cohorts within the program\n- ProgramCohort -> Program (child): Each cohort belongs to a program\n- ProgramCohort -> ProgramCohortMember (parent): Cohorts contain member records linking to participants\n- ProgramCohortMember -> ProgramCohort (child): Each member record belongs to a cohort\n- ProgramCohortMember -> Account (child): Each member references a participant account\n- BenefitDisbursement -> Benefit (child): Each disbursement delivers a specific benefit\n- BenefitDisbursement -> Account (child): Each disbursement goes to a participant account\n- BenefitDisbursement -> BenefitSession (lookup): Disbursements may be linked to scheduled benefit sessions\n- BenefitSchedule -> Benefit (child): Each schedule defines delivery timing for a benefit\n- BenefitSchedule -> BenefitSession (parent): Schedules generate individual benefit sessions\n- BenefitSession -> BenefitSchedule (child): Each session is generated from a benefit schedule\n- BenefitSession -> BenefitDisbursement (parent): Session attendance creates disbursement records\n- BenefitAssignment -> Account (child): Each assignment references a participant account\n- BenefitAssignment -> Benefit (child): Each assignment references a specific benefit\n- GoalDefinition -> OutcomeDefinition (lookup): Goals connect to outcome definitions for measurement\n- GoalDefinition -> CarePlan (lookup): Care plans reference goal definitions for participant-level tracking\n- GoalDefinition -> Program (lookup): Goals are associated with programs they measure\n- Case -> Account (child): Each case references a participant account\n- Case -> CarePlan (parent): Cases can have personalized care plans\n- Case -> CaseReferral (parent): Cases can be created from referrals\n- Case -> InteractionSummary (parent): Cases have interaction summaries documenting meetings and notes\n- CarePlan -> Account (child): Each care plan serves a participant account\n- CarePlan -> Case (child): Care plans are linked to cases\n- CarePlan -> CarePlanTemplate (child): Care plans are built from templates\n- CarePlan -> GoalDefinition (lookup): Care plans reference goal definitions for tracking progress\n- CarePlanTemplate -> CarePlan (parent): Templates are used to create care plan instances\n- CarePlanTemplate -> CarePlanTemplateBenefit (parent): Templates include pre-configured benefit assignments\n- CarePlanTemplate -> CarePlanTemplateGoal (parent): Templates include pre-configured goal definitions\n- CarePlanTemplateBenefit -> CarePlanTemplate (child): Each template benefit belongs to a care plan template\n- CarePlanTemplateBenefit -> Benefit (child): References the benefit to be included\n- CarePlanTemplateGoal -> CarePlanTemplate (child): Each template goal belongs to a care plan template\n- CarePlanTemplateGoal -> GoalDefinition (child): References the goal definition to track\n- CaseReferral -> Case (child): Each referral is linked to a case\n- CaseReferral -> Account (lookup): Referrals reference the participant account\n- AssessmentTask -> Account (child): Each assessment is completed for a participant\n- AssessmentTask -> DiscoveryFramework (child): Assessments use a configured discovery framework\n- AssessmentTask -> Case (lookup): Assessments are linked to participant cases\n- DiscoveryFramework -> AssessmentTask (parent): Frameworks are used to create assessment task instances\n- InteractionSummary -> Account (child): Each interaction is linked to a constituent account\n- InteractionSummary -> Case (lookup): Interactions can be linked to a case for case management context\n- OutcomeDefinition -> Program (lookup): Outcomes link to the programs that drive them\n- OutcomeDefinition -> Indicator (parent): Outcomes are measured through indicators\n- OutcomeDefinition -> GoalDefinition (lookup): Outcomes connect to program goals\n- Indicator -> OutcomeDefinition (child): Each indicator measures a specific outcome\n- Indicator -> IndicatorResult (parent): Indicators accumulate result data points over time\n- IndicatorResult -> Indicator (child): Each result belongs to an indicator\n- IndicatorResult -> Program (lookup): Results are collected from program activities\n- VolunteerInitiative -> JobPosition (parent): Initiatives are staffed by job positions\n- VolunteerInitiative -> Program (lookup): Volunteer initiatives support program delivery\n- Position -> JobPosition (parent): Generic positions are customized as job positions for specific initiatives\n- Position -> Competency (lookup): Positions may require specific competencies\n- JobPosition -> VolunteerInitiative (child): Each job position belongs to a volunteer initiative\n- JobPosition -> Position (child): Each job position references a generic position\n- JobPosition -> JobPositionShift (parent): Job positions have one or more shifts\n- JobPositionShift -> JobPosition (child): Each shift belongs to a job position\n- JobPositionShift -> JobPositionAssignment (parent): Shifts have volunteer assignments\n- JobPositionAssignment -> JobPositionShift (child): Each assignment belongs to a shift\n- JobPositionAssignment -> Account (child): Each assignment references a volunteer account\n- Competency -> PersonCompetency (parent): Competencies are held by individuals through person competency records\n- Competency -> Position (lookup): Positions may require specific competencies\n- PersonCompetency -> Account (child): Each person competency belongs to a volunteer account\n- PersonCompetency -> Competency (child): References the competency definition\n- Examination -> PersonExamination (parent): Examinations are taken by individuals through person examination records\n- PersonExamination -> Account (child): Each exam result belongs to a volunteer account\n- PersonExamination -> Examination (child): References the examination definition\n- PersonLocationAvailability -> Account (child): Each location availability belongs to a volunteer account\n- FundingOpportunity -> GrantApplication (parent): Funding opportunities receive grant applications\n- FundingOpportunity -> Program (lookup): Funding opportunities may be tied to specific programs\n- GrantApplication -> FundingOpportunity (child): Each application is for a specific funding opportunity\n- GrantApplication -> Account (child): Applications reference the applicant organization account\n- GrantApplication -> ApplicationReview (parent): Applications are evaluated through review records\n- GrantApplication -> FundingAward (parent): Approved applications receive funding awards\n- ApplicationReview -> GrantApplication (child): Each review evaluates a specific application\n- Budget -> GrantApplication (child): Each budget belongs to a grant application\n- Budget -> FundingAward (lookup): Approved budgets inform funding award amounts\n- FundingAward -> GrantApplication (child): Each award results from an approved application\n- FundingAward -> Account (child): Awards are linked to the grantee organization\n- FundingAward -> OutcomeDefinition (lookup): Awards require grantees to report on defined outcomes\n- ActionPlan -> ActionPlanTemplate (child): Each plan is created from a template\n- ActionPlan -> ActionPlanItem (parent): Plans contain task items to be completed\n- ActionPlanTemplate -> ActionPlan (parent): Templates are used to create action plan instances\n- ActionPlanTemplate -> ActionPlanTemplateItem (parent): Templates contain template item definitions\n- ActionPlanTemplateItem -> ActionPlanTemplate (child): Each item belongs to a template\n- ActionPlanItem -> ActionPlan (child): Each item belongs to an action plan\n- StageDefinition -> ActionPlanTemplate (lookup): Stage transitions can trigger action plan templates\n- StageDefinition -> VolunteerInitiative (lookup): Stages track volunteer initiative lifecycle progress\n\n## Cross-Domain Connections\n- Constituents <-> Households & Groups: Individuals belong to households\n- Constituents <-> Relationships: Constituents have relationships\n- Constituents <-> Contact Information: Accounts have contact information\n- Constituents <-> Fundraising Pipeline: Donors are constituents\n- Constituents <-> Gift Transactions: Gift transactions belong to donors\n- Constituents <-> Gift Entry & Processing: Donors matched during gift entry\n- Constituents <-> Soft Credits: Soft credits attributed to constituents\n- Constituents <-> Program Management: Program participants are constituents\n- Constituents <-> Case Management: Case subjects are constituents\n- Constituents <-> Volunteer Management: Volunteers are constituents\n- Constituents <-> Grantmaking: Grant applicants are constituents\n- Constituents <-> Security & Access: Constituent data access is secured\n- Constituents <-> Setup & Configuration: Account model configured in setup\n- Households & Groups <-> Relationships: Household members have inter-relationships\n- Households & Groups <-> Contact Information: Households share contact information\n- Households & Groups <-> Campaigns & Outreach: Household addressing for campaign mailings\n- Relationships <-> Soft Credits: Relationships inform soft credit decisions\n- Relationships <-> Grantmaking: Organization relationships inform grants\n- Relationships <-> Setup & Configuration: Relationship types configured in setup\n- Contact Information <-> Campaigns & Outreach: Contact points used for campaign outreach\n- Fundraising Pipeline <-> Gift Commitments: Opportunities become commitments\n- Fundraising Pipeline <-> Gift Transactions: Opportunities produce transactions\n- Fundraising Pipeline <-> Campaigns & Outreach: Campaigns source fundraising\n- Fundraising Pipeline <-> Gift Designations: Opportunities have default designations\n- Fundraising Pipeline <-> Soft Credits: Opportunity contact roles drive soft credits\n- Fundraising Pipeline <-> Agentforce & Einstein AI: AI generates gift proposals\n- Fundraising Pipeline <-> Action Plans: Action plans drive cultivation steps\n- Fundraising Pipeline <-> Setup & Configuration: Fundraising settings in setup\n- Fundraising Pipeline <-> Grantmaking: Grants use the fundraising pipeline\n- Gift Commitments <-> Gift Transactions: Commitments generate transactions\n- Gift Commitments <-> Gift Entry & Processing: Gift entry creates commitments\n- Gift Commitments <-> Gift Designations: Commitments allocated to designations\n- Gift Commitments <-> Campaigns & Outreach: Commitments attributed to campaigns\n- Gift Transactions <-> Gift Entry & Processing: Gift entry creates transactions\n- Gift Transactions <-> Gift Designations: Transactions linked to designations\n- Gift Transactions <-> Soft Credits: Transactions have soft credits\n- Gift Transactions <-> Campaigns & Outreach: Transactions attributed to campaigns\n- Gift Entry & Processing <-> Gift Designations: Designations applied during entry\n- Gift Entry & Processing <-> Campaigns & Outreach: Campaign and source code set during entry\n- Gift Entry & Processing <-> Agentforce & Einstein AI: AI assists gift data entry\n- Gift Designations <-> Campaigns & Outreach: Campaigns have default designations\n- Gift Designations <-> Outcome Management: Designation data feeds impact reporting\n- Gift Designations <-> Setup & Configuration: DPE calculates designation rollups\n- Campaigns & Outreach <-> Action Plans: Campaigns trigger action plans\n- Campaigns & Outreach <-> Agentforce & Einstein AI: AI provides campaign insights\n- Campaigns & Outreach <-> Setup & Configuration: DPE aggregates campaign metrics\n- Program Management <-> Outcome Management: Programs measured by outcomes\n- Program Management <-> Case Management: Programs serve case participants\n- Program Management <-> Grantmaking: Grants fund programs\n- Program Management <-> Volunteer Management: Volunteers support programs\n- Program Management <-> Action Plans: Action plans automate program steps\n- Program Management <-> Setup & Configuration: Program settings in setup\n- Program Management <-> Agentforce & Einstein AI: AI summarizes program benefits\n- Case Management <-> Outcome Management: Case outcomes tracked\n- Case Management <-> Agentforce & Einstein AI: AI summarizes notes\n- Case Management <-> Action Plans: Action plans automate case steps\n- Case Management <-> Security & Access: Case data requires access controls\n- Case Management <-> Setup & Configuration: Case settings in setup\n- Outcome Management <-> Grantmaking: Outcomes support grant reporting\n- Volunteer Management <-> Action Plans: Action plans automate volunteer onboarding\n- Volunteer Management <-> Agentforce & Einstein AI: AI assists volunteer matching\n- Volunteer Management <-> Setup & Configuration: Volunteer settings in setup\n- Grantmaking <-> Agentforce & Einstein AI: AI summarizes grant applications\n- Grantmaking <-> Action Plans: Action plans support grant review workflows\n- Security & Access <-> Setup & Configuration: Permissions configured in setup\n\n## Data Flows\n### Constituents\n1. Configure the Account model in setup, enable required constituent features, and secure record access with Salesforce permissions and sharing.\n2. Create individuals as Person Accounts and organizations or groups as Business Accounts, then associate individuals with households and maintain account contact information.\n3. Connect constituents through relationships, Group Memberships, and account hierarchies.\n4. Classify donors with Contact Profiles, match them during gift entry, associate gift transactions with them, and attribute soft credits to appropriate constituents.\n5. Use constituent records as program participants, case subjects, and volunteers across Nonprofit Cloud.\n6. Register individual or organizational grant applicants as constituents for grantmaking processes.\n### Households & Groups\n1. Create or identify the individual constituent records.\n2. Create the household account and its Party Relationship Group.\n3. Add household members with roles, active status, and membership dates.\n4. Maintain member inter-relationships and any overlapping group affiliations.\n5. Update shared and constituent-specific contact information.\n6. Use current household addressing data for campaign mailings.\n### Relationships\n1. Configure active relationship types and reciprocal labels in setup.\n2. Connect constituents through individual, household, and organizational relationship records.\n3. Assign the appropriate role and record current status or start-date details where available.\n4. Review household-member and organization connections in constituent profiles and relationship graphs.\n5. Use relationship context to inform soft credit decisions, fundraising engagement, and grantmaking work.\n### Contact Information\n1. Identify the constituent or household account that owns the contact information.\n2. Create address, email, and phone contact point records for the account.\n3. Classify each contact point and designate the appropriate primary record.\n4. Maintain seasonal and undeliverable address details as circumstances change.\n5. Keep shared household contact information current for household members.\n6. Use current contact points for constituent service and campaign outreach.\n### Fundraising Pipeline\n1. Identify donor constituents and source fundraising asks from campaigns or grant solicitation work.\n2. Create opportunities, assign default designations, relate constituent contacts, and organize cultivation tasks with Action Plans.\n3. Advance opportunities through the configured Sales Process while using analytics and Agentforce-generated gift proposals to support engagement.\n4. Close successful opportunities and connect them to gift commitments with the appropriate campaign and designation allocations.\n5. Process the commitments into gift transactions and create soft-credit recognition from the relevant opportunity contact roles.\n6. Configure and maintain the required Fundraising settings in Setup.\n### Gift Commitments\n1. Convert an opportunity into a gift commitment or create the commitment through gift entry.\n2. Attribute the commitment to a campaign and allocate its amount to gift designations.\n3. Define recurring or custom commitment schedules with amounts and effective dates.\n4. Process the schedules to generate and update related gift transactions.\n5. Apply lifecycle changes and record their attribution in the Change Attribution Log.\n### Gift Transactions\n1. Advance fundraising activity and identify the constituent account that made the gift.\n2. Process the gift through gift entry to create or update its GiftTransaction record.\n3. Apply the transaction to a gift commitment when it fulfills a scheduled or outstanding payment.\n4. Attribute the transaction to campaigns and allocate its amount among gift designations.\n5. Add gift soft credits for constituents who influenced the donation, then update payment and acknowledgment status as processing proceeds.\n6. Create a full or partial refund against a paid transaction when needed, preserving the complete transaction history.\n### Gift Entry & Processing\n1. Identify or create the donor during gift entry, using Agentforce for Nonprofit Cloud assistance where applicable.\n2. Capture the amount, payment method, campaign, outreach source code, designations, and soft credits.\n3. Create a gift commitment or match the entry to an eligible commitment and scheduled transaction.\n4. Save and process a standalone entry, or collect entries in a Gift Batch through the grid or CSV import.\n5. Run a batch dry run, review processing results, and correct entries with errors.\n6. Process the entries to create gift transactions and update the related commitment fulfillment records.\n### Gift Designations\n1. Create active gift designations and select the organization-wide default.\n2. Assign default designations to campaigns, opportunities, and gift commitments.\n3. Inherit the applicable defaults when commitments and gift entries are created or matched.\n4. Review or override allocations during gift entry and transaction processing.\n5. Create transaction designation records that split each payment by amount and percentage.\n6. Run Data Processing Engine rollups and use designation results in fundraising and impact reporting.\n### Soft Credits\n1. Record the donor’s Gift Commitment or Gift Transaction.\n2. Use constituent relationships and opportunity contact roles to identify parties that influenced the gift.\n3. Select each credited constituent and assign the appropriate CreditType role.\n4. Enter the soft-credit Amount or percentage on the commitment, transaction, or gift entry.\n5. Process the gift to create transaction-level GiftSoftCredit records and update fundraising summaries.\n### Campaigns & Outreach\n1. Define the campaign, its financial details, default designations, audience, and required action plans.\n2. Select household addressing and contact-point information for campaign outreach.\n3. Create campaign-specific Outreach Source Codes for the channels, messages, and audiences used.\n4. Apply a valid source code during gift entry to populate the campaign and source Fundraising records.\n5. Link gift commitments and opportunities to campaigns, then attribute resulting gift transactions.\n6. Run Data Processing Engine summaries and review campaign metrics, reports, and Agentforce insights.\n### Program Management\n1. Configure Program Management settings in Nonprofit Cloud Setup.\n2. Create grant-funded programs, benefits, benefit types, units of measure, schedules, sessions, and goal definitions.\n3. Enroll constituent and case participants, organize them into cohorts, and automate program steps with action plans.\n4. Assign scheduled or ad hoc benefits and record attendance and benefit disbursements.\n5. Measure programs through linked outcomes and coordinate volunteer support for service delivery.\n6. Summarize program benefits with Agentforce and report participant, cohort, and delivery activity.\n### Case Management\n1. Enable case referrals, care plans, assessments, and interaction summaries in setup, then assign permissions and access controls.\n2. Receive an inbound or outbound constituent referral and convert it into a case for assessment and follow-up.\n3. Complete discovery and assessment tasks, and use action plans to automate required case steps.\n4. Create a template-based care plan and use the case to guide benefits, goals, and program enrollment.\n5. Record confidential interaction notes and use Agentforce to summarize participant history and outstanding actions.\n6. Track case outcomes and evaluate participant progress through connected outcome management records.\n### Outcome Management\n1. Define intended outcomes and organize them within the impact strategy or theory of change.\n2. Link the outcome strategy to programs, benefits, and case-related work.\n3. Configure indicators with baseline values, target values, and collection frequencies.\n4. Collect dated indicator results with the relevant measurement context.\n5. Evaluate program and case outcomes against the defined measures.\n6. Report impact for stakeholders, grant reporting, and fundraising designations.\n### Volunteer Management\n1. Enable Volunteer Management in Setup, assign permissions, and configure locations, operating hours, and assignment defaults.\n2. Create volunteer constituents as Person Accounts and record their competencies, examinations, and location availability.\n3. Define Volunteer Initiatives that support programs, then add reusable Positions, specific Job Positions, qualifications, and shifts.\n4. Apply Action Plans to automate volunteer onboarding tasks and publish eligible opportunities to the Experience Cloud portal.\n5. Match volunteers by qualifications, availability, and location, using Agentforce for Nonprofit Cloud when appropriate, and create assignments.\n6. Track assignment status and actual hours, monitor shift coverage, and report volunteer metrics.\n### Grantmaking\n1. Create a FundingOpportunity with its funding amount, application details, eligibility criteria, deadline, status, and publication setting.\n2. Publish eligible opportunities in the Experience Cloud grantee portal for prospective applicants to discover.\n3. Capture each request as a GrantApplication tied to an organization account, using organization relationships for context and Agentforce to summarize application information.\n4. Collect budgets and supporting documents, then coordinate ApplicationReview and approval work with action plans.\n5. Approve the budget, create the FundingAward, track disbursements, and connect funded work to programs.\n6. Measure grant progress through outcomes, and manage grants received by the organization through the inbound fundraising pipeline.\n### Agentforce & Einstein AI\n1. Enable Einstein generative AI, Agentforce, and the required Nonprofit Cloud features.\n2. Configure summary components, agent templates, messages, permissions, and subagents.\n3. Ground requests in Salesforce fundraising, program, case, grantmaking, volunteer, campaign, and gift-entry data.\n4. Generate gift proposals, program and note summaries, grant summaries, and board-ready application versions.\n5. Use agents to research donors, surface campaign insights, assist gift entry, and match volunteers to uncovered shifts.\n6. Review generated results, regenerate or copy summaries as needed, and complete the related business workflow.\n### Action Plans\n1. Define an active Action Plan Template for the target business process.\n2. Add ordered template items with task descriptions, priorities, timing offsets, and dependencies.\n3. Apply or trigger the plan for fundraising cultivation, program steps, case workflows, volunteer tasks, campaign actions, or grant review workflows.\n4. Assign generated action plan items to users and calculate their due dates from the plan start date.\n5. Track item statuses, plan status, completion percentage, and applicable record stages.\n6. Complete the assigned tasks and advance the target record through its configured process.\n### Security & Access\n1. Identify the Nonprofit Cloud features and data each user needs to access.\n2. Assign any required permission set licenses in Setup.\n3. Combine managed, custom, and muting permission sets in the appropriate permission set groups.\n4. Assign permission sets or permission set groups to individual users or multiple users.\n5. Configure sharing and external-access permissions to protect constituent records and control case data access.\n6. Review assignments when features or organization-specific customizations change.\n### Setup & Configuration\n1. Select the Person Account or Contact and Business Account model, then configure group memberships and relationship types.\n2. Assign setup, feature, object, field, and flow permissions through the applicable permission sets and security configuration.\n3. Enable the required Nonprofit Cloud features and configure program, volunteer, case, and stakeholder settings.\n4. Configure Fundraising thresholds, recurring processing, donor matching, picklists, record types, and sales processes.\n5. Enable Data Pipelines, then clone, activate, and run the required Data Processing Engine definitions.\n6. Schedule Data Processing Engine jobs to refresh designation rollups, campaign metrics, donor scores, program summaries, and segmentation data.\n\n## Links\nUse these URLs when mentioning domains or components:\n- [Constituents](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/nonprofitcloud/#/constituents)\n- [Households & Groups](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/nonprofitcloud/#/households)\n- [Relationships](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/nonprofitcloud/#/relationships)\n- [Contact Information](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/nonprofitcloud/#/contact_points)\n- [Fundraising Pipeline](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/nonprofitcloud/#/fundraising)\n- [Gift Commitments](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/nonprofitcloud/#/gift_commitments)\n- [Gift Transactions](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/nonprofitcloud/#/gift_transactions)\n- [Gift Entry & Processing](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/nonprofitcloud/#/gift_entry)\n- [Gift Designations](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/nonprofitcloud/#/designations)\n- [Soft Credits](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/nonprofitcloud/#/soft_credits)\n- [Campaigns & Outreach](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/nonprofitcloud/#/campaigns)\n- [Program Management](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/nonprofitcloud/#/programs)\n- [Case Management](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/nonprofitcloud/#/case_management)\n- [Outcome Management](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/nonprofitcloud/#/outcomes)\n- [Volunteer Management](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/nonprofitcloud/#/volunteers)\n- [Grantmaking](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/nonprofitcloud/#/grantmaking)\n- [Agentforce & Einstein AI](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/nonprofitcloud/#/agentforce)\n- [Action Plans](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/nonprofitcloud/#/action_plans)\n- [Security & Access](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/nonprofitcloud/#/security)\n- [Setup & Configuration](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/nonprofitcloud/#/setup)\n";
